# The Next Generation of Platinum Drugs: Targeted Pt(II) Agents, Nanoparticle Delivery, and Pt(IV) Prodrugs

## abstract
The platinum drugs, cisplatin, carboplatin, and oxaliplatin, prevail in the treatment of cancer, but new platinum agents have been very slow to enter the clinic. Recently, however, there has been a surge of activity, based on a great deal of mechanistic information, aimed at developing nonclassical platinum complexes that operate via mechanisms of action distinct from those of the approved drugs. The use of nanodelivery devices has also grown, and many different strategies have been explored to incorporate platinum warheads into nanomedicine constructs. In this Review, we discuss these efforts to create the next generation of platinum anticancer drugs. The introduction provides the reader with a brief overview of the use, development, and mechanism of action of the approved platinum drugs to provide the context in which more recent research has flourished. We then describe approaches that explore nonclassical platinum(II) complexes with trans geometry or with a monofunctional coordination mode, polynuclear platinum(II) compounds, platinum(IV) prodrugs, dual-threat agents, and photoactivatable platinum(IV) complexes. Nanoparticles designed to deliver platinum(IV) complexes will also be discussed, including carbon nanotubes, carbon nanoparticles, gold nanoparticles, quantum dots, upconversion nanoparticles, and polymeric micelles. Additional nanoformulations, including supramolecular self-assembled structures, proteins, peptides, metal-organic frameworks, and coordination polymers, will then be described. Finally, the significant clinical progress made by nanoparticle formulations of platinum(II) agents will be reviewed. We anticipate that such a synthesis of disparate research efforts will not only help to generate new drug development ideas and strategies, but also will reflect our optimism that the next generation of approved platinum cancer drugs is about to arrive.

## CONTENTS
## 1. INTRODUCTION
Platinum anticancer agents represent one of the great success stories in the field of medicinal inorganic chemistry. They highlight the confluence of serendipity and rational design in drug development. Three platinum-containing drugs are approved worldwide for treating cancer in humans, cisplatin, carboplatin, and oxaliplatin (section 3.1). An additional three are approved for use in specific countries, and they are nedaplatin, lobaplatin, and heptaplatin (section 3.1). Despite having been introduced to the market almost 40 years ago, platinum complexes remain among the most widely used anticancer chemotherapeutics. One notable mark of the success of the platinum drugs is the fact that, since the introduction of cisplatin into the treatment regimen of testicular cancer patients, cure rates for this disease have exceeded $95\%$ .1 The clinical relevance of these drugs is further underscored by the fact that carboplatin is listed on the World Health Organization’s Model List of Essential Medicines. Moreover, in the 2009 Ambulatory Care Drug Database maintained by the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, platinum complexes as a class were listed in the medical charts of
American patients with a frequency surpassed only by five other anticancer drugs (methotrexate, raloxifene, medroxyprogesterone, tamoxifen, and leuprolide).3 The clinical trials database maintained by the U.S. National Institutes of Health (NIH), which lists $>186000$ clinical trials in over 180 countries, cites cisplatin as a component in more active clinical trials than any other anticancer agent (Figure 1).4 Similar trends hold for the European Union Clinical Trial Register, which is maintained by the European Medicines Agency (EMA) and lists over 25 000 trials with a European clinical trials database (EudraCT) protocol,5 as well as the International Clinical Trials Registry Platform of the WHO.6 Despite the widespread use of these drugs, a new platinum agent has not received worldwide approval in over a decade. Research activity into new platinum anticancer agents has remained intense, however,7 as this Review will demonstrate.
Our discussion begins with a brief description of the mechanism of action of the classical platinum drugs. For more comprehensive treatments, the reader is referred to several excellent reviews and monographs.7−12 Here, we provide sufficient mechanistic background information for appreciating the discussions that follow. We then discuss platinum(II) complexes that lead to cancer cell death by the same mechanism as the three classical platinum drugs but that enjoy enhanced activity due to molecular targeting. This section is followed by coverage of platinum(II) complexes that operate by different mechanisms. Because a very large number of complexes have been prepared that fall in these two categories, emphasis is given to those having validated targeting properties and/or well-established mechanisms. Coverage of platinum(IV) prodrugs that release cisplatin, carboplatin, oxaliplatin, or a close analog upon reduction in the cell follows next. A subset of these complexes not only release an active platinum molecule, but also provide an additional bioactive substance that can function in a manner orthogonal to that of the platinum(II) agent, serving as “dual-threat” drug candidates. A small number of platinum(IV) complexes appear to act by mechanisms distinct from that of the prodrug family, and they are covered next. An extensive treatment of the nanodelivery of platinum complexes is then provided, with a focus on two nanoparticulate formulations that have shown the greatest progress in clinical trials.13 The organization of this Review thus reflects the structures and mechanisms of the compounds (Figure 2).
## 2. MECHANISM OF ACTION
The mechanism by which the classical platinum drugs elicit an anticancer effect has been the subject of decades of investigation. Numerous experiments and trials conducted by chemists, biologists, and physicians have afforded a framework under which we can explain the data that have been obtained from compounds analogous to cisplatin, that is, complexes that are neutral and square-planar with two cis $\mathsf{a m}(\mathbf{m})\mathbf{\bar{\Lambda}} $ and two cis anionic ligands.14,15 The $\mathsf{a m}(\mathbf{m})\mathbf{ine}$ ligands can be chelating or nonchelating and are referred to as the “nonleaving group ligands” because they remain bound to the metal center throughout the course of intracellular transformations. In contrast, the “leaving group” ligand(s), which can be monodentate anionic or chelating dianionic fragments, leave the platinum(II) coordination sphere. The generalized mechanism of action involves four key steps (Figure 3): (i) cellular uptake, (ii) aquation/activation, (iii) DNA binding, and (iv) cellular processing of DNA lesions leading to cell death.15
The two pathways by which cisplatin is most likely taken up by cells are passive diffusion through the plasma membrane and active transport mediated by membrane proteins.16 The small size and planar geometry of cisplatin have long been cited as supportive of passive diffusion. Consistent with this conclusion, cisplatin uptake does not saturate with increasing concentration and is proportional to the administered concentration.17−19 Furthermore, structural analogs of cisplatin do not inhibit uptake of the drug.20 Conversely, other studies suggest that active transport is the predominant mechanism of uptake. For instance, cisplatin uptake has been linked to expression levels of copper transporters.16,21,22 In a similar manner, oxaliplatin efficacy has been linked to expression of organic cation transporters (OCTs).23 Also, reactive aldehydes can inhibit cisplatin uptake, presumably by reacting with membrane proteins that internalize cisplatin.24 A combination of both passive and active transport best explains the data accrued to date, but the relative importance of these pathways and the extent to which they influence each other remain to be determined.
The square-planar geometry of cisplatin facilitates ligand substitution, which is necessary for it to form the DNA lesions that characterize its activity. Cisplatin undergoes a ligand substitution event prior to DNA binding in which a chloride ligand is replaced by a water molecule. Extrapolation of results from simple aqueous model solutions indicates that such aquation is suppressed in the bloodstream, where the chloride ion concentration is high $\left(\sim100\ \mathrm{mM}\right)$ , but occurs more readily in the cytoplasm, where the chloride ion concentration falls below $\dot{2}0\:\:\mathrm{~mM}$ 25 In the presence of these lower salt concentrations, the half-life of the aquation reaction producing cis- $\mathrm{\small{[Pt(NH_{3})_{2}C l(H_{2}O)]^{+}}}$ is approximately $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ The positive charge on the platinum complex can attract it to the negatively charged nuclear DNA. Carboplatin and oxaliplatin feature chelating ligands opposite the firmly bound $\mathsf{a m}(\bar{\mathbf{m}})\mathrm{ine}$ groups. These chelating ligands are substituted by water much more slowly, and solutions of these two drugs are stable to aquation over a period of weeks to months.26−30
Aquated cisplatin can enter the nucleus, and the metalcoordinated water molecule can be substituted by a heterocylic DNA base. The strongest early evidence that confirmed DNA to be the primary target of platinum drugs was the sensitivity of cells deficient in DNA repair to treatment with these compounds.31 Although decades of research have supported the hypothesis that nuclear DNA is the functional target of platinum drugs, interactions with proteins and RNA have also been proposed to play a role.32−34 The most nucleophilic sites of DNA are the N7 atoms of purine residues guanine and adenine, and these are preferentially platinated. $^{\mathrm{{\dot{19}5}}}\mathrm{{Pt}}$ NMR spectroscopic monitoring experiments revealed that cisplatin first forms monofunctional adducts on DNA.35 Following this event, the remaining chloride ligand is substituted for a second guanine base, forming a cross-link on the DNA. Such crosslinks can occur between deoxyguanosines on the same strand or on different strands, giving rise to intrastrand and interstrand DNA cross-links, respectively. The $1,2\mathrm{-d}(\mathrm{GpG})$ intrastrand cross-link is the most prevalent lesion $(65\%)$ , but 1,2-(ApG) $(25\%)$ and $1,3{\mathrm{-d}}(\mathrm{GpT}\bar{\mathrm{pG}})$ $(10\%)$ intrastrand cross-links also form along with small amounts of GG interstrand crosslinks.36,37 Similar cross-links are formed by carboplatin and oxaliplatin, but the relative proportions vary.38,39 These DNA adducts distort the structure of DNA in a substantial manner. Early studies provided evidence of bending and unwinding of the double helix upon platination.40 Atomic resolution structures of many of the adducts formed by different platinum anticancer agents are now available (Figure 4).
Cells with DNA that has been damaged in this manner arrest at the ${\bf G}2/{\bf M}$ transition of the cell cycle and attempt to repair the platinated DNA.10 Cisplatin lesions are most effectively removed by the nucleotide excision repair (NER) machinery, and enhanced NER can produce cells that resist platinum treatment.41,42 The repair machinery must, however, be able to access the damage. If proteins not involved in DNA repair bind to a platinum lesion, they can effectively shield it from repair. One class of proteins that are involved in such “repair shielding” comprises the high-mobility group (HMG) box proteins that recognize and bind distorted DNA. The distortion that a cisplatin lesion induces in the DNA double helix resembles that which is recognized by certain HMG proteins.43 The HMGB proteins are among the most abundant proteins in the nucleus,44 and display a particular affinity for $\{\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}\}^{2+}$ $1,2\mathrm{-d}(\mathrm{GpG})$ intrastrand cross-links.45 The ability of these proteins to shield platinum adducts from repair might contribute to the cisplatin sensitivity of cancer cells that overexpress them, but the previously ignored redox state dependence of the platinated DNA−protein interaction confounds an interpretation of the results present in the literature.46 The ability of cisplatin to cure testicular cancer might be related to the fact that testes cells express the HMGB4 isoform of this protein,47 and experiments to evaluate this possibility are ongoing. If the cell is unable to repair the platinum-DNA damage, the expression of pro-apoptotic proteins increases, prompting release of cytochrome c and activation of intracellular caspases.10 These proteases effectively degrade the cell in a process of programmed cell death known as apoptosis. One of the main mechanisms by which the cell is signaled to trigger apoptosis in response to platinum treatment is the inhibition of transcription past platinum lesions by RNA polymerases.48
A number of deactivation pathways exist that can sequester platinum complexes or otherwise prevent them from triggering apoptosis (Figure 5).49 Because the current platinum drugs are all administered intravenously, they can interact with blood components, one of which is human serum albumin (HSA). HSA, the most abundant protein in the human bloodstream, contains a cysteine residue that can interact with systemically administered metal complexes.50 The main interaction of cisplatin with HSA, however, appears to involve sulfur donors other than cysteine thiols, the thioether side chains of methionine residues.51
Once inside the cell, sulfur-rich metallothioneins can sequester platinum complexes, as can glutathione.41 As part of the cellular detoxification program, dedicated export pumps remove glutathione-xenobiotic adducts from the cytoplasm. Overexpression of these pumps, including ATP7B, has been implicated in cisplatin resistance.
3. PLATINUM(II) COMPOUNDS WITH A MECHANISM OF ACTION SIMILAR TO THAT OF CISPLATIN
## 3.1. Approved Platinum Drugs
The first experiments showing that platinum complexes could have anticancer activity are rooted in the serendipitous observations made by Rosenberg and co-workers when studying the influence of electric fields on bacterial cell divisio n.52−55 We refer the interested reader to a detailed and highly readable account of the experiments and circumstances that led to the clinical use of cisplatin in the 20th century (Chart 1),56 over 100 years after the original synthesis of the compound.57 These works led to the first human patient being treated with cisplatin in 1971.58 Approval for marketing cisplatin was first obtained in Canada in 1978, followed soon after by approval in the United States and then elsewhere across the world (Table 1).58 Cisplatin is currently used primarily to treat testicular, ovarian, and bladder cancers, but has also been used in the treatment of head and neck cancers, lung cancer, malignant pleural mesothelioma, neuroblastoma, tumors of the brain, and esophageal and cervical cancers.59 The need for large amounts of precious metal starting materials for the synthesis of cisplatin inspired precious metal refining companies to become involved early in the drug discovery process.
The initial clinical development of cisplatin was fostered by a collaboration between the National Cancer Institute (NCI), Johnson Matthey, and Engelhard Industries, the latter two being precious metal companies based in the United Kingdom and United States, respectively.56 Johnson Matthey continued to pursue a research program into platinum anticancer agents and, in collaboration with Bristol-Myers, the Institute for Cancer Research, and the Royal Marsden Hospital, developed carboplatin (Chart 1).60 The success of carboplatin, originally known as JM8, has largely been driven by its favorable toxicity profile.61 This feature derives directly from alteration in reactivity at the metal center because of the chelating nature of the leaving group ligand and, potentially, the conformation that this ligand assumes.12,62 Carboplatin is used primarily to treat ovarian cancer but has also found use in treating retinoblastomas, neuroblastomas, nephroblastomas, and brain tumors, as well as cancers of the head and neck, endometrium, cervix, testes, breast, lung, and bladder.63
The discovery and development of nedaplatin (Chart 1) by Shionogi Pharmaceutical Co. was carried out entirely in Japan, currently the only country in which it has regulatory approval, which was granted in 1995.58,64 This drug, initially referred to as 254-S, features cis ammine nonleaving group ligands like cisplatin and carboplatin. The chelating leaving group ligand is glycolate, which confers greater water solubility $\mathrm{\bar{(10~mg~mL^{-1})}}$ ) than the two chloride ligands of cisplatin $(2.5\mathrm{\mg}\mathrm{~mL^{-1}},$ ).
Nedaplatin is primarily used to treat cancers of the head and neck and esophagus as well as small cell lung cancer and nonsmall cell lung cancer.13,61 Clinical trials exploring the expanded use of nedaplatin are ongoing.13,64
Heptaplatin was developed by Sunkyong Industry Research Center in Korea under the name SKI 2053R. It entered clinical trials in the 1990s and received approval from the Korean Food and Drug Administration in 1999. It is currently marketed under the name SunPla for the treatment of gastric cancer. The compound features malonate as a chelating leaving group ligand, as well as a chelating 2-(1-methylethyl)-1,3-dioxolane4,5-dimethanamine. The nonleaving group ligand forms a seven-membered chelate ring, giving the drug its generic name. The two stereocenters in the ligand have $R$ stereochemistry, and, although we have not been able to find any publications that describe the reason as to why this stereoisomer was chosen, by analogy to oxaliplatin (vide infra) the opposite enantiomer and meso compound most likely have lower activity.
Lobaplatin can be viewed as a derivative of heptaplatin in which a cyclobutane ring is fused to the seven-membered chelate ring instead of a functionalized dioxolane. Lobaplatin, however, is formulated as a racemic mixture of the $^{R,R}$ and $^{\sc}$ enantiomers of the nonleaving group ligand. Because only Slactate is used as the leaving group ligand, the marketed drug is a mixture of diastereomers.65 The compound was initially developed by ASTA Medica in Germany under the name D19466, but was later acquired by the German company Zentaris AG, a subsidiary of the Canadian biopharmaceutical company Æterna Zentaris. Zentaris eventually sold Hainan Tianwang (Chang’an) International Pharmaceutical the rights to manufacture and market the drug in China.66 Although clinical trials were initially carried out in Europe, the United States, Australia, Brazil, and South Africa to examine efficacy in patients with a range of different cancers, regulatory approval was only obtained in China. Lobaplatin is approved primarily for the treatment of chronic myelogenous leukemia, but is also used in patients suffering from small cell lung cancer and metastatic breast cancer.66 Although literature sources and press releases indicate that lobaplatin had already received regulatory approval in $2003,^{13,66,^{\bullet}67}$ the Chinese FDA State Food and Drug Administration Database lists the approval year as 2010.68
Oxaliplatin, occasionally referred to as l-OHP, is the most recent platinum anticancer drug to have gained international approval for marketing.69 This drug was first synthesized in Japan, but was subsequently developed and approved in France and the United States. Oxaliplatin is now a component of the front-line combination chemotherapy treatment for colon cancer.7 Oxaliplatin features a chelating oxalate leaving group ligand and a chelating R,R-diaminocyclohexane (DACH) nonleaving group ligand.70 DACH ligands have long been investigated as components in platinum anticancer agents.60 In the case of oxaliplatin, empirical evidence revealed that the $^{R,R}$ stereoisomer was more effective than the enantiomeric $^{\sc}S,\cal{S}$ isomer or the related meso compound with cis amine groups.71 The origin of this difference came to light in later crystallographic studies that revealed the $^{R,R}$ isomer preferentially forms a hydrogen bond between a pseudoequatorial NH hydrogen atom of the R,R-DACH ligand and the O6 atom of the $3^{\prime}$ -dG of the platinated $\mathsf{d}(\bar{\mathbf{G}}\mathsf{p}\mathbf{G})$ lesion.72 Such an interaction is not possible for the enantiomeric complex. The exceptional activity of oxaliplatin in colon cancer has been linked to the ability of this drug to act as a substrate for the OCTs, which are overexpressed in a large proportion of colon cancer patients.23
## 3.2. The Next Generation of Cisplatin-Like Platinum(II) Complexes
Many researchers are making increasingly complex perturbations to the general molecular framework shared by the approved platinum drugs in the quest to uncover novel mechanisms of cell killing, alter the spectrum of activity, and render new cancers susceptible to platinum therapy. Such endeavors are described in subsequent sections of this Review. Here, we discuss efforts to create novel platinum $(\mathrm{II})$ complexes that are structurally similar to the approved drugs and are expected to operate by a comparable mechanism of action. As indicated above, early medicinal chemistry efforts produced many compounds of the form cis- $\cdot\mathrm{PtA}_{2}\mathrm{X}_{2},$ where the A group is ammine or a substituted amine and X is an anionic ligand or $X_{2}$ is a chelating dianionic ligand. The SciFinder search tool maintained by the Chemical Abstracts Service lists over 4700 distinct compounds with this general formulation that are classified as antitumor agents, and a comprehensive discussion of these compounds is beyond the scope of this Review. We suspect that this number is a significant underestimate of the true extent of development that has occurred across both academia and industry.
Rather than review all cisplatin derivatives, we have chosen to focus in depth on the incorporation of targeting units into platinum(II) agents of known anticancer activity. Such efforts seek to finally realize the Magische Kugeln that Ehrlich sought over 100 years ago.73 This concept of a drug as a magic bullet that seeks out its target is well-matched with constructs bearing targeting units that direct platinum warheads to cancer cells by interacting with receptors overexpressed on the surfaces of these cells.74 The concept can be extended to encompass targeting of the tumor as a whole instead of cancer cells themselves by seeking proteins expressed on angiogenic blood vessels or allowing selective activation within the acidic or hypoxic tumor microenvironment.75 Finally, targeting can also take place at the subcellular level, whereby platinum can be directed to specific organelles to elicit distinct biological effects. 3.2.1. Glucose Receptor Targeting of Platinum(II). Carbohydrates can engage in extensive hydrogen-bonding interactions, a feature exploited in biological systems to achieve high fidelity recognition.76 Such recognition has been proposed as a paradigm for drug targeting.77 Another facet of carbohydrate biology that can be exploited for drug targeting is the enhanced uptake of glucose by cancer cells.78 To supply the energy required for cell division that is characteristic of cancer, malignant cells require much greater levels of glucose.79 The need for glucose is driven by the altered metabolic state in which many cancer cells exist, a manifestation of the Warburg effect, details of which are provided in section 6.80 This enhanced uptake of glucose relies on overexpression of glucose membrane transporters, such as GLUT1−4, and has been exploited in the use of $^{18}\mathrm{F}$ 2-fluoro-2-deoxy-D-glucose for positron emission tomography imaging of tumors.81,82 Although many examples of platinum complexes bound to a variety of sugars are known, little evidence has been gathered to suggest that these carbohydrate motifs enhance the activity of the anticancer agent by interacting with a specific receptor.
Analogs of cisplatin have been prepared in which the ammine ligands are replaced by aminosugars. Early studies demonstrated that complexes with 2-amino-2-deoxy-D-glucopyranose coordinated to platinum could be prepared but displayed no significant anticancer activity.83−87 Using 2,3-diaminosugars, complexes analogous to oxaliplatin were obtained that exhibited promising activity in vitro and in animal models. For instance, $\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}$ (2,3-diamino-2,3-dideoxy-D-glucose) (Chart 2A) given as a $50~\mathrm{mg~kg^{-1}}$ intraperitoneal injection was able to more than double the survival time of mice bearing a sarcoma 180.88 Although cisplatin can have a similar effect at a much lower dose $({\bar{8}}\ \mathrm{mg}{\mathrm{\kg}}^{-1})$ , this dose approaches the MTD ( $\mathrm{i}3\mathrm{\mg}$ $\mathbf{kg}^{-1},$ ). Substitution of the halide leaving group ligands in the diaminodideoxyglucose platinum(II) complexes for oxalate or malonate produced less active species, and studies investigating the reactivity of these compounds with dGMP are consistent with the slower rate of reaction expected from a chelating leaving group ligand.89 A similar reduction in activity was observed by incorporating the bidentate dicarboxylate ligand of carboplatin, CBDCA, into the platinum(II) complex of methyl 2,3-diamino-2,3-dideoxy-L-xylopyranoside.90 Although one of the justifications for pursuing 2,3-diaminoglucose complexes is the similarity between this ligand and the 1,2-diaminocyclohexane of oxaliplatin, it is important to note that the conformation of the D-glucopyranose ring results in the diamine chelate ring adopting a $\lambda$ conformation. The stereochemistry of the $R,R-$ DACH in oxaliplatin results in a $\delta$ conformation of this chelate ring.70 This feature of oxaliplatin has been suggested to be the origin of the greater activity of complexes of R,R-DACH as opposed to S,S-DACH.71,72,91 Although this discrepancy is noted in passing in one publication,92 its effects are clearly seen, but not discussed, in later work.89 We anticipate that the use of L-glucose in the preparation of the diaminoglucose would result in a more active platinum complex as a result of its ability to more closely mimic oxaliplatin. The use of L-glucose might appear to run contrary to the motivation of using a sugar that can be recognized by the cellular uptake machinery, but substitution of alcohols for amines, and their chelation of a metal, will most likely inhibit specific recognition and transport to an equivalent or greater extent than the use of the mirror image of the natural glucose enantiomer.
We briefly note, in relation to the discussion above, that a pair of enantiomeric platinum complexes bearing chiral 2R,3Sdiaminocamphor ligands was investigated, and, as in the development of oxaliplatin, in vitro assays revealed one enantiomer to be significantly more cytotoxic than the other.93 The amino substituents were arranged cis to one another (2R,3S) providing a meso framework and differing chirality at the 1-position produced the two enantiomers investigated. These enantiomers, (1R,2R,3S) and (1S,2R,3S), do not differ in the orientation of hydrogen atoms of the amino substituents, and so the origin of the difference in cytotoxicity must differ from that of enantiomeric trans-1,2-DACH platinum complexes.93
Returning to the discussion of targeted platinum−sugar complexes, a linker can also be inserted between the sugar unit and the platinum-binding amine, as in the case of cisdichloro[(2- $\beta$ -D-glucopyranosidyl)propane-1,3-diamine]- platinum(II) (Chart 2B).94 Glycosylation increases water solubility without compromising anticancer activity. In an analogous system, a (2S)-2,3-diamino-1-propanol linker was attached to D-glucose (Chart 2C), L-glucose, D-galactose, Dxylose, or D-mannose and subsequently complexed to a platinum center.95 The authors found a difference between the activities of the D- and L-glucose conjugates and suggest that interaction with a specific receptor might a play role in the increased activity of the D-glucose conjugate. Although platinum−sugar conjugates had been explored for almost two decades by the time this report was published,95 it is one of the earliest instances in which interaction of the sugar moiety with a specific receptor is proposed to enhance activity. Linkage through an ethylenediamine was also performed with Dgalactose and D-ribose, but the activity of the complexes was not investigated.96
Glucose can alternatively be incorporated into a leaving group ligand such as malonate, but an early study reported no increase in activity for such a construct over the analogous carboplatin.97,98 A larger set of complexes with a range of different sugars was prepared, but the results of biological assays with these complexes have yet to be released.99 Readers interested in a comprehensive overview of platinum−glucose conjugates are referred to a recent review.100 Here, we focus our discussion on platinum−glucose conjugates for which experiments have been performed to explore the uptake mechanism.
The first evidence that conjugation of a glucose unit with a platinum complex can exploit the glucose receptor was presented for a construct bearing a DACH nonleaving group and a glucose-functionalized malonate leaving group as ligands (Chart 2D).101 In vitro cytotoxicity assays in the presence of phlorizin, which blocks glucose transport, revealed that the inhibitor decreased the efficacy of the platinum−glucose conjugate, consistent with a model in which glucose transporters mediate uptake of the complex.101 In the studies listed above, glucose was never attached to the platinum center through the 6 position, perhaps because of the synthetic difficulty of performing this modification. Analysis of the crystal structure of a bacterial homologue of GLUT1 bound to Dglucose revealed, however, that the hydroxyl group at this position is the only one that does not have a hydrogen-bonding interaction with protein side chains.102 Platinum(II) complexes with a DACH nonleaving group and a malonate leaving group ligand attached to glucose at the 6 position by a linker of variable length were prepared and shown to be taken up selectively by cancer cells (Chart 2E).103 Studies with different glucose transport inhibitors confirmed that cellular uptake was dependent on glucosylation and directly impacted cell-killing efficacy. An effect of chain length on uptake was observed, and modeling studies indicate that an overly long linker between the glucose and platinum inhibits the ability of transporters such as GLUT1 to undergo the conformational change required to ferry the construct across the cell membrane. Organic cation transporters also play a role in the uptake and efficacy of the most cytotoxic glucoconjugate prepared in this study.103
3.2.2. Steroid Targeting of Platinum(II): Estrogen and Testosterone. Another class of targeted platinum(II) complexes comprises those in which a steroid unit has been incorporated into the nonleaving group ligand. These steroids can act as targeting units that direct the platinum agent to tissues expressing the cognate steroid receptor. The estrogen receptor (ER), for instance, is an established oncology target because this protein is overexpressed on the surfaces of some cancers, particularly breast cancer, where it stimulates cell proliferation.104−107 The monoclonal antibody trastuzumab, also known as Herceptin, binds to and interferes with the signaling of this receptor.108 It is important to note that, in addition to the classical estrogen receptor, now designated $\operatorname{ER}\alpha,$ a novel estrogen receptor, $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{ER}\beta,}\end{array}$ , has been more recently discovered.109−111 $\bar{\mathrm{ER}}\beta$ might play an important role in cancer progression by exhibiting antiangiogenic and antiproliferative properties.112 A steroid unit capable of interacting with the ER can be linked to a platinum center to influence the anticancer activity of the metal complex by either interfering with the biological function of the receptor or permitting enhanced uptake of the platinum complex. Several recent reviews have comprehensively summarized the early developments in this area.74,113,114 One of the earliest studies that investigated platinum complexes conjugated to steroids involved those with phosphine nonleaving group ligands and a catecholate leaving group functionalized with steroids for use in metalloimmunoassays. Although tested in vitro for cell killing activity, these compounds did not fare better than cisplatin.115−117 Dozens of platinum(II) complexes bearing estrogen derivatives at either the leaving group or nonleaving group ligands have since been reported,113 but most have not been tested to ensure that a specific interaction with the ER is operative and leads to enhanced activity by one of the two mechanisms described above. In one report that does take such measures, two compounds, cis-dichloro[N-(4-(17-ethynylestradiolyl)-benzyl)- ethylenediamine]platinum(II) (Chart 3A) and cis-diamino[2- (4-(17-ethynylestradiolyl)-benzoylamino)-malonato]platinum(II) (Chart 3B), agonize the ER at low concentrations, leading to enhanced proliferation.118 Higher concentrations, conversely, exhibited cytotoxicity.118 Such a bimodal effect renders these particular compounds unsuitable for further investigation as cytotoxic anticancer agents.
A series of estradiol conjugates were prepared, linked to the platinum center through a spacer attached to an $N.$ -functionalized 2-aminoalkylpyridyl chelate (Chart 3C). Although they did not show any apparent enhanced toxicity in $\operatorname{ER}+$ as compared to $\mathrm{ER-}$ cells, most compounds in the series bound to $\mathtt{E R}\alpha$ with very high affinit y.119−121 Some members of the series also displayed high affinity for $\mathrm{ER}\beta.^{12}$ 2 As the length of the aliphatic chain that links the estradiol and the platinum complex is increased, the solubility of the complexes decreases, which the authors report as a significant shortcoming. Use of a poly(ethylene glycol) (PEG) chain (Chart 3D), however, allows the length to be varied without compromising solubility.123 Enhanced cytotoxicity in $\mathrm{ER}+$ cell lines was observed for certain PEG chain lengths, and these results were rationalized using molecular modeling methods. This steroidtargeting strategy was also applied to derivatives of carboplatin and oxaliplatin124 and recently extended with the design and synthesis of a $17\beta$ -acetyl-testosterone conjugate linked to platinum through its $7\alpha$ position.125 In the same way that estrogen units can target $\mathrm{ER}+$ cancer cells, testosterone can target platinum to cancer cells expressing the androgen receptor (AR). Activity was observed in both $\mathrm{AR}+$ and AR− prostate cancer cell lines.125 Mechanistic studies established that the compounds induce S-phase arrest and double-stranded DNA breaks. Antitumor studies using a chick chorioallantoic membrane xenograft assay confirmed the ability of these compounds to inhibit tumor growth.
A platinum(II) complex with an ethylenediamine nonleaving group ligand functionalized with a ligand for the ER was able to interact with the estrogen receptor even after binding to a 16- mer DNA duplex (Chart 3E).126 Although the steroid conjugate binds DNA to a lesser extent than an analog lacking an ER ligand, the former is more toxic to cells. Enhanced toxicity was observed in cell lines deficient in DNA repair, strongly suggesting that DNA damage is the means by which cell death is induced. The authors propose that DNA repair shielding or steroid receptor hijacking might be operative.
Nonsteroidal estrogen mimics have also been linked to platinum compounds. The first compounds of this sort to be prepared, a series of stereoisomers of dichloro[1,2-bis(4- hydroxyphenyl)ethylenediamine]platinum(II), competed with estrogen for interaction with the ER, but were toxic to $\mathrm{ER}+$ and ER− cells alike.127 Substitution at the 2 and 6 positions of the two phenyl rings with chlorine atoms, which has been shown to increase the affinity of the free ligand for the $\mathrm{ER},^{128}$ produced a set of complexes that not only interacted with the ER but also selectively killed $\mathrm{ER}+$ mammary carcinoma cells.129 Variation of the substituent on the nitrogen atoms and ring carbon atoms can influence estrogenicity and cytotoxicity, but often in a mutually exclusive manner.130
3.2.3. Steroid Targeting of Platinum(II): Bile Acids. Platinum(II) complexes have also been conjugated to members of the steroid acids known as bile acids in an effort to direct compounds to the liver, because hepatic epithelial cells express a number of transport proteins that take up bile salts from the bloodstream.131 Initial work in this area is reported in a set of papers describing the preparation of a series of platinum(II) complexes ligated to DACH and 2 equiv of a bile acid (e.g., hyodeoxycholate, Chart 4A).132,133 The lability of monodentate carboxylates bound to platinum(II) centers almost certainly assures that, upon dissolution, the complex very rapidly forms a distribution of aquated species in dynamic equilibrium. Although activity was observed in these studies, additional work does not appear to have been pursued by these authors.
A related series of compounds named Bamet, a portmanteau of bile acid and metal, was prepared that also features bile acids attached to a platinum warhead. The first generation complex, Bamet-H2 (Chart 4), was prepared simply by allowing sodium cholylglycinate to react with tetrachloroplatinate(II). The product, formulated as a monoanionic sodium salt on the basis of conductivity data, was cytostatic against L1210 murine leukemia and displayed enhanced uptake by the liver as compared to cisplatin.134 The compounds Bamet-R1 and Bamet-R2 (Chart 4) were prepared by treating cisplatin with sodium cholylglycinate.135−137 These complexes are presumably able to form $\left\{\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}\right\}^{2+}$ cross-links on nuclear DNA following cellular uptake and shedding of the bile acid by aquation.138 Although active, the cytotoxicities of these compounds, and those of the related bis(ursodeoxycholate) complex Bamet-UD2 (Chart 4) and [(cis-dichloro(3-(3- aminopropylamine)propyl)glycocholamideplatinum(II)] (Bamet-D3, Chart 4), were lower than that of cisplatin in cultured cell lines.139,140 Related complexes with trans geometry were even less active.141 Given the propensity of bile acids to form liposomes, it is not surprising that a liposomal formulation of Bamet-R2 could be readily prepared.136 In a parallel line of investigation, analogs of these complexes in which the nonleaving group ligand is ethylenediamine were studied because they are fluorescent and have increased emission upon binding to DNA and release of the leaving group ligands.142−145 The mechanism of action and many of the in vivo properties of these compounds, notably those related to their special affinity for liver tissue (hepatic organotropism), have been reviewed.146 Early studies with rat hepatocytes and isolated rat livers confirmed that Bamet-R2 is taken up by the pathway naturally used for bile acid uptake and is secreted into the bile.147 These cholephilic characteristics were also observed in experiments with live rats.148 Close to a decade of subsequent preclinical experiments have produced a set of results, suggesting that these compounds might merit clinical investigation for the treatment of hepatic malignancy.146
A bile acid with a chelating dicarboxylate motif bound to a cis-diammineplatinum(II) fragment was explored as an orally administered anticancer agent.149 Preliminary in vitro assays revealed activity in cultured murine hepatoma cells. A syngeneic orthotopic rat model of hepatocellular carcinoma confirmed that the complex had antitumor activity.149 An alternative means of linking a bile acid to a platinum(II) center involves conjugation to the nonleaving group ligands, a strategy that has afforded complexes with activity in cultured cells by a mechanism of action similar to that of cisplatin.150,151 Other terpenoids, the class of molecules to which steroids and bile acids belong, have also been conjugated to platinum(II) complexes in an effort to direct the cytotoxic agent to cancer cells,152−155 although in many instances it remains to be determined whether the enhanced cellular uptake observed for these complexes arises from specific interactions with membrane receptors or whether their inherent lipophilicity simply enhances trans-membrane diffusion.
3.2.4. Steroid-Related Targeting of Platinum(II). The peripheral benzodiazepine receptor, also known as TSPO,156 is thought to be involved in regulating the transport of cholesterol and the synthesis of steroids, although recent evidence has called the latter role into question.157 The protein has been suggested as a viable target for directing cancer therapeutics,158 and it is overexpressed in a number of tumor tissues.159−161 Platinum(II) complexes chelated by a functionalized bidentate thiazolylimidazopyridine interact strongly with this receptor.162 Although these complexes can be taken up by cultured cancer cells that express TSPO, they exhibit weak anticancer activity. Alteration of the thiazolyl ring to generate a monodentate ligand, and addition of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ to the vacated coordination site, produced complexes with enhanced potency.163 Radioligand binding assays confirmed the ability of the complex to interact with, and presumably be taken up by, TSPO. Biological studies confirmed that treatment with the platinum complex induced apoptosis.
3.2.5. Folate Targeting of Platinum(II). A number of different cancer cell lines and cells derived from the tumors of patients display an overexpression of a glycoprotein that acts as a folate receptor (FR).164 Folic acid contains the pteroic acid unit and is vital to a number of central biochemical pathways, including those related to DNA synthesis. To satisfy rapid cell growth, folate uptake is enhanced in cancer cells, and the folate motif could be used to target a platinum complex to them.165 In comparison to sugars and steroids, folates have been used sparingly as targeting agents for platinum(II) complexes. An early study of the interaction of cisplatin with cellular folates involved the isolation of the complex that results from substitution of the chloride ligands with tetrahydrofolate.166 Although this complex is an inhibitor of dihydrofolate reductase and the folate transport system, the lack of labile coordination sites suggests that it will not be able to operate as a cytotoxic agent in a manner analogous to that of cisplatin.166 Preparation of a variety of cisplatin and carboplatin derivatives bearing a folate unit conjugated to either the nonleaving group ligands or the leaving group ligands (Chart 5A) established much of the
## Chart 5. Chemical Structures of Folate-Targeted Platinum(II) Complexes
chemistry required to develop platinum complexes with the capacity to target the FR.167 Unfortunately, the low water solubility of these molecules prohibited their use in biological experiments. The use of a PEG spacer between a dicarboxylate chelator and a folic acid unit (Chart 5B) affords enhanced water solubility, and mechanistic studies showed that the conjugate is taken up by FR-mediated endocytosis.168 The conjugate was, however, less cytotoxic than carboplatin, a feature that appears to stem directly from the formation of fewer platinum-DNA adducts. As described in section 8, folate targeting has been successfully used to direct platinum-loaded nanoparticle drug delivery vehicles to FR-expressing cancer cells.
3.2.6. Peptide Targeting of Platinum(II). The earliest report of a platinum(II)−peptide conjugate involved attaching a platinum warhead to derivatives of the minor groove binding agents netropsin and distamycin (Chart 6A).169 The peptide was able to enhance platination of poly(dA) tracts over poly(dG) tracts, the latter of which are preferentially platinated by cisplatin. The ability to explore a much wider range of platinum $(\mathrm{II})$ −peptide conjugates arose when synthetic strategies compatible with solid-phase peptide synthesis were developed.170−173 This chemistry involved linking the platinum complex to the peptide through a functionalized ethylenediamine nonleaving group ligand. The platinum chemistry could be performed on the solid support, and the complex survived harsh deprotection and cleavage conditions. This technology was used to screen dozens of platinum(II)−peptide conjugates in vitro for anticancer activity, although no significant hits were obtained in this screen.174 These peptide conjugates exhibit a reduced capacity to platinate DNA and reduced cytotoxicity. The DNA sequence specificity of platination was generally unaltered with different peptide sequences.175 We note briefly, however, that use of individual charged amino acids, such as ornithine, lysine, or arginine, in place of the nonleaving group ligands can alter this specificity.176 Highly complex peptide architectures were also conjugated to platinum(II) using these methods, including conjugates of octreotide analogs.17
Although the opportunities offered by peptide-based targeting are significant, instances in which targeting has been successfully achieved with platinum(II) conjugates are few. More examples exist with platinum(IV) constructs and nanodelivery vehicles as described below. One well-characterized example involves the use of a malonate nonleaving group ligand attached to a PEGylated cyclic peptide through a linker (Chart 6B).178 The cyclic peptide, c(CNGRC), presents the Asn-Gly-Arg sequence that targets the CD13 receptor overexpressed on the surface of certain cancer cells.179 The targeted complex was more toxic to prostate cancer cells expressing CD13 than nontargeted carboplatin, and competition assays confirmed that the complex is taken up via interaction with CD13. Fluorescence microscopy studies and DNA fragmentation assays are consistent with apoptosis as the mechanism of cell death.
Another example involves the subcellular targeting of active platinum(II) units to the mitochondria. Following initial work on the anticancer activity of cationic platinum(II) complexes with ammine nonleaving group ligands and $\beta$ -diketonate leaving group ligands,180 an analogous complex with a succinylacetonate ligand was prepared. The pedant carboxylate of this complex was used to form an amide bond to the Nterminus of a mitochondrial penetrating peptide (Chart 6C).181 The decapeptide, ${\mathrm{r}}(\operatorname{F}_{x}\operatorname{r})_{3},$ where r is D-arginine and $\mathrm{F}_{x}$ is Lcyclohexylalanine, is nontoxic, protease resistant, and mitochondrial penetrating due to its lipophilic and cationic nature.182,183 Conjugation of the platinum complex to the peptide was carried out on the solid support. Fluorescence microscopy confirmed localization of the conjugate to the mitochondria of cultured ovarian cancer cells, and PCR amplification studies indicate that, in contrast to treatment with cisplatin, mitochondrial DNA is platinated preferentially over nuclear DNA. Even though the location of platination differs, the platinum−peptide conjugate is still able to induce apoptosis. Mouse embryonic fibroblasts that are deficient in their proofreading of mitochondrial DNA were more sensitive to the treatment, an observation consistent with a shift in the target of the platinum complex from nuclear DNA to mitochondrial DNA.
4. PLATINUM(II) COMPOUNDS WITH A MECHANISM OF ACTION DIFFERENT FROM THAT OF CISPLATIN
Early structure−activity relationship (SAR) studies revealed that altered geometry can drastically change the chemotherapeutic activity of a platinum complex. Whereas cisplatin displays excellent activity against some cancer types, transdiamminedichloroplatinum(II) lacks activity.60,184 Two major factors are thought to contribute to the contrasting activities of the cis and trans stereoisomers. The trans disposition of the two chloride ligands in trans-diamminedichloroplatinum(II) renders them kinetically labile in comparison to those of the cis isomer, and thus susceptible to undesirable side reactions with extra- and intracellular biomolecules.185−188 Additionally, the major cytotoxic DNA lesion formed by cisplatin, the 1,2- intrastrand cross-link between adjacent purine bases, is s t e r e o c h e m i c a l l y i n a c c e s s i b l e t o t r a n s - d i a m m i n e d i c h l o r o p l a t i n u m ( I I ) . I n s t e a d , t r a n s - diamminedichloroplatinum $(\mathrm{II})$ forms 1,3-intrastand cross-links (ca. $28\%$ ) and monofunctional adducts (ca. $60\%$ ), which undergo conversion to interstrand cross-links (ca. $12\%$ ), preferentially between guanine and a complementary cytosine.189 In cells, however, very few interstrand cross-links are formed because of the slow transformation of monofunctional adducts and 1,3-intrastand cross-links to interstrand crosslinks.190 DNA adducts formed by trans-diamminedichloroplatinum(II) do not halt DNA replication as efficiently as those formed by cisplatin and are prone to effective DNA repair.191
## 4.1. trans-Platinum(II) Complexes
The discrepancy in cisplatin and trans-diamminedichloroplatinum(II) activity led to the early belief that only platinum complexes with cis leaving groups were endowed with antitumor activity.60 The development of biologically active trans-diamminedichloroplatinum(II) analogs, however, has dispelled this notio n.185−188 Active trans-platinum complexes can be divided into the following subtypes: (i) transplatinum(II) complexes with heteroaromatic ligands, (ii) trans-platinum(II) complexes with iminoether ligands, and (iii) trans-platinum(II) complexes with asymmetric aliphatic amine ligands.
4.1.1. trans-Platinum(II) Complexes with Heteroaromatic Ligands. Substitution of the ammine ligand(s) in transdiamminedichloroplatinum(II) with bulky, planar N-donor ligands affords trans-platinum(II) complexes with high in vitro cytotoxicity, equivalent to their corresponding cis isomers and cisplatin.192−194 Some analogs, such as trans- $\bar{\mathrm{[PtCl}_{2}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})}$ - L] and trans- $\left[\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2}\right]$ where $\mathrm{~L~}=$ pyridine, quinoline, isoquinoline, thiazole, or benzothiazole (Chart 7), display therapeutically significant activities in cisplatin- and oxaliplatinresistant cell lines.194,195 According to NCI human tumor panel screening studies and COMPARE algorithm196 analyses, transplatinum(II) complexes of this type exhibit a spectrum of activity that differs significantly from that of any other anticancer agent in the NCI database.195 Their unique cytotoxicity profiles are attributed to their structural and DNA-binding properties.185 The type and distribution of DNA lesions induced by such trans-platinum(II) complexes is distinctly different from those of cisplatin, transdiamminedichloroplatinum(II), and other cis-platinum(II) agents.197,198 The presence of bulky planar ligands increases the propensity for monofunctional adduct formation and subsequent interstrand cross-linking. Monofunctional adducts formed by members of the trans- $\left[\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})\mathrm{L}\right]$ series, depicted in Chart 7, on short duplex DNA induce conformational changes similar to those produced by cisplatin.199 In vitro studies in cultured breast cancer cells showed that these complexes formed DNA-topoisomerase I cross-links that are capable of triggering DNA strand breaks and apoptosis.185,200 Such ternary DNA−protein cross-links are not observed following treatment with cisplatin200 and could explain, in part, the distinctive cellular response evoked by transplatinum(II) complexes with bulky planar ligands.
4.1.2. trans-Platinum(II) Complexes with Iminoether Ligands. trans-Diamminedichloroplatinum(II) analogs with one or two iminoether ligands exhibit promising biological activity.201−203 Iminoether ligands exist as isomers because of different possible $E$ and $Z$ configurations about the $\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{N}$ bond. As a result, iminoether complexes of platinum(II) produce not only cis and trans isomers, but also exhibit additional isomerism at the coordinated ligand.204,205 The transEE- $\scriptstyle\left[\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}(\mathrm{HN=C(OMe)Me})_{2}\right]$ complex was the first member of the series to be studied in detail (Chart 8). This complex displays inhibitory effects against a panel of cancer cell lines comparable to those of cisplatin.205 Furthermore, this compound exhibits no cross-resistance with cisplatin in ovarian cancer cells and substantial in vivo activity in P388 leukemiabearing mice.206,207 The cellular uptake and degree of DNA platination were significantly higher for iminoether bearing trans-platinum(II) complexes than cisplatin, which suggested that DNA was the main intracellular target.207 The complexes form stable monofunctional adducts with duplex DNA,208,209 which bend the DNA toward the minor groove.210 The monofunctional adducts were not recognized by HMGB proteins, and thus were readily removed by NER.210 Conversion of the monofunctional adducts into DNA−protein cross-links, however, produced lesions that were able to bypass NER, inhibit DNA polymerases, or lead to cell death. SAR studies established that trans-platinum(II) analogs with one iminoether ligand were less toxic than those with two such ligands.203 Within the trans- $\scriptstyle\left[\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}\left(\mathrm{HN=C(OMe)Me}\right)$ $(\bar{\mathrm{NH}}_{3})]$ series (Chart 8), the $E$ configuration exhibited greater inhibitory potency against cancer cells than the $Z$ configuration, indicating that iminoether ligand configuration is a major determinant of activity. To systematically study the effect of iminoether ligand configuration on trans-platinum $(\mathrm{II})$ activity, trans-platinum(II) complexes bearing cyclic iminoether ligands mimicking the $E$ and $Z$ configuration were prepared. Cyclic ligands avoid complications that arise from the $\scriptstyle{E-Z}$ isomerization encountered in acyclic iminoether compounds.211
M e c h a n i s t i c s t u d i e s r e v e a l e d t h a t , l i k e t r a n s - diamminedichloroplatinum(II), the trans- ${\big[}{\mathrm{PtCl}}_{2}{\big(}{\mathrm{HN}}{=}{\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{.}}$ - $\big(\mathrm{OMe}\big)\mathrm{Me}\big)\big(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\big)\hat{]}$ series formed monofunctional adducts that developed into interstrand cross-links between adjacent guanine and cytosine bases.203,212 The trans- $[\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}(\mathrm{HN}=$ $\mathbf{\bar{C}}(\mathrm{OMe})\mathbf{Me})(\mathbf{\dot{NH}}_{3})]$ lesions have been likened to a flexible hinge, inducing structural effects on DNA different from the more rigid trans-diamminedichloroplatinum(II) lesion.203 More recently, another generation of platinum complexes mimicking iminoether derivatives was investigated. These trans-platinum(II) complexes bearing one or two ketamine ligands (acetonimine) exhibited micromolar toxicity against cancer cells and circumvented cisplatin resistance in ovarian cancer cell lines (A2780cisR and 41McisR).213
4.1.3. trans-Platinum(II) Complexes with Asymmetric Aliphatic Amine Ligands. Aliphatic amine ligands have been employed to generate asymmetric platinum(II) agents, such as trans- $\left[\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}\right.$ (isopropylamine)L] where $\mathrm{~L~}=$ dimethylamine, isopropylamine, or propylamine (Chart 9), that display cytotoxicity against cancer cells with multifactorial cisplatin resistance and ras oncogene overexpression.214,215 In a fashion similar to trans-diamminedichloroplatinum(II), trans$\left[\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}\right.$ (isopropylamine)(dimethylamine)] forms interstrand cross-links between guanine and a complementary cytosine, but the quantity of lesions formed is 3-fold higher for the former.216 The ability of the latter complex to form interstrand cross-links over a relatively short period of time is claimed to be the major contribution to overcoming ras-related resistance. trans-Platinum $(\mathrm{II})$ complexes with one aliphatic amine ligand, such as trans- $\left[\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}(\mathrm{N}\bar{\mathrm{H}}_{3})\mathrm{L}\right]$ where $\mathrm{L}=2$ -methyl-butylamine or sec-butylamine, have been prepared with the aim of improving the water solubility of the parent bis(aliphatic amine) complexes.217 The second generation complexes retained the cytotoxicity profile of the original series, including the ability to form efficient interstrand cross-links and bypass cisplatin resistance.
## 4.2. Polynuclear Platinum(II) Compounds
Polynuclear platinum agents that share similarities with transplatinum(II) complexes account for another class of pharmacologically active platinum-based anticancer agents.218−221 S uch compounds contain trans- $\{\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\}$ units with bridging alkanediamine linkers of variable length, designed to facilitate long-distance, flexible intrastrand and interstrand cross-links (Chart 10). Such lesions cannot be formed by traditional mononuclear platinum(II) agents like cisplatin and trans-diamminedichloroplatinum(II). The diplatinum complex trans- $[(\mathrm{PtCl}(\mathrm{NH_{3}})_{2})_{2}^{-}\mu$ - $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{(CH}_{2}\mathrm{)}_{4}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{)}\mathrm{]Cl}_{2}$ forms 1,2-, 1,3-, and $^{1,4}$ -interstand cross-links between guanines on opposite strands.222,223 In 1,3- and 1,4-cross-links, the guanines are separated by one and two base pairs, respectively, whereas the 1,2-cross-link is formed between guanines of neighboring base pairs.222 These unconventional DNA adducts enable the diplatinum complex to overcome cisplatin resistance in ovarian cancer cells.220 To improve the DNA binding ability of the diplatinum(II) complex, trinuclear platinum $(\mathrm{II})$ complexes were prepared by incorporating a third platinum center within the alkanediamine linker.224 After systematic SAR
Chart 10. Chemical Structures of Di- and Trinuclear Platinum Agentsa
studies, bis[trans-diamminechloroplatinum $\left(\operatorname{II}\right)\operatorname{J}[\mu$ -transdiamminebis(hexanediamine)platinum(II)] nitrate (BBR3464) was selected for preclinical development. BBR3464 is taken up in large amounts by cancer cells and forms characteristic DNA cross-links, which mediate its cytotoxic effects.225,226 The major DNA adduct formed is the 1,4-interstand cross-link. This lesion induces directional bending of DNA and local unwinding of the helix. Moreover, this lesion evades removal by NER. The DNA lesions formed by BBR3464 were not detected by antibodies raised against cisplatin-modified DNA but were recognized by antibodies raised against trans-diamminedichloroplatinum(II)- modified DNA, indicating that BBR3464 might exhibit greater mechanistic similarities with trans-platinum(II) complexes.224 Other studies claim that BBR3464 induces DNA damage by triggering B-to-Z and B-to-A transitions in DNA conformation.227,228 In vitro and in vivo toxicity studies showed that BBR3464 is up to 3 orders of magnitude more potent than cisplatin and is able to overcome cisplatin resistance in several types of cancer.218,225,229−231 The fact that BBR3464 acts independently of p53, a tumor suppressor protein that is mutated, defective, or inactivated in several cancers, suggests that it holds great therapeutic potential. The major drawback of BBR3464 is its inherent systemic toxicity. Phase I clinical trials revealed that the MTD is as low as $0.\dot{1}2~\mathrm{mg~m}^{-2}~\mathrm{day}^{-1}$ on a daily, five-dose schedule.232 Upon escalating the dose to 0.17 $\mathrm{mg^{'}m^{-2}\ d a y^{-1}}.$ , severe myelosuppression and gastrointestinal toxicity were observed. On the other hand, low urinary excretion and low nephrotoxicity were reported. Phase II trials in patients with ovarian cancer, small cell lung cancer, nonsmall cell lung cancer, gastric, and gastroesophageal adenocarcinoma produced mixed results.233−236 Although BBR3464 displayed a distinct lack of activity in gastric and small cell lung cancers, it showed better activity in nonsmall cell lung cancer patients and advanced ovarian cancer patients failing platinum-taxane regimens. In the latter case, BBR3464 displayed hints of activity deserving of further evaluation (16 partial responses out of 79 patients). The results of two Phase II trials launched over 10 years ago by Theradex, for the treatment of patients with locally advanced or metastatic pancreatic cancer and small cell lung cancer, have yet to be released.
Noncoordinating multinuclear platinum(II) agents based on BBR3464 exhibit strong DNA binding affinity and anticancer activity. These agents were generated by replacing the labile chloride ligands on BBR3464 with ammine groups (TriplatinNC) or extended amine groups (TriplatinNC-A) (Chart 10).237 X-ray crystallographic studies revealed that TriplatinNC and TriplatinNC-A bind noncovalently to DNA through electrostatic and hydrogen-bonding interactions. The terminal platinum units form discrete amine-phosphate-ammine binding motifs called “phosphate clamps” within the minor groove, which induce B-to-A and B-to-Z conformational changes in canonical DNA sequences.237−239 The conformational change cooperatively enhances the interaction of minor-groove binders like Hoechst 33258, and remains unperturbed in the presence of intercalators, such as ethidium bromide.240,241 Recent work has shown that TriplatinNC and TriplatinNC-A can also condense DNA and induce aggregation of small tRNA molecules, due to the highly cationic nature of the platinum complexes.242,243 Furthermore, these agents inhibit topoisomerase-I-mediated relaxation of supercoiled DNA. In light of these findings, the biological mechanism of action of TriplatinNC and TriplatinNC-A is thought to involve nucleic acid condensation or aggregation, with inhibitory effects on topoisomerase-I enzymatic activity.242
Biological studies showed that TriplatinNC and TriplatinNC-A display micromolar toxicity against cisplatin-sensitive and cisplatin-resistant ovarian cancer cells.244 The ability of the agents to overcome cisplatin resistance was attributed to their high cellular accumulation, presumably because of their cationic nature and their unique mode of binding to DNA. Molecular biology assays showed that the downstream cellular responses evoked by TriplatinNC and TriplatinNC-A in mastocytoma cells were markedly different.245 Although TriplatinNC induces caspase-mediated apoptosis reliant on the function of p53 and the pro-apoptotic protein BAX, in a manner similar to cisplatin and BBR3464, TriplatinNC-A induces cell death in a manner that is independent of p53- or BAX-status. Given the role of p53 and BAX in cisplatin-mediated cell death, TriplatinNC-A could hold significant clinical value if the results can be recapitulated with in vivo models.
## 4.3. Noncovalently Binding Platinum(II) Complexes
Mononuclear platinum(II) compounds that bind to DNA noncovalently have also shown promising antineoplastic properties and are gaining increasing support as potential alternatives to conventional platinum drugs.246−251 Metallointercalators with $\pi$ -conjugated heterocyclic ligands, such as bipyridine, terpyridine, and phenanthroline, utilize $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and dipole−dipole interactions to intercalate between base pairs in double-stranded DNA.252−254 Metallointercalators can unwind, bend, and distort DNA topology, and it is their structural effect on DNA that is thought to mediate their antiproliferative properties. Systematic studies of charged platinum(II) complexes of general formula $\mathrm{\big[Pt\big(I_{L}\big)\big(A_{L}\big)\big]^{2+},}$ where $\mathrm{I_{L}}$ is an intercalating ligand and $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{L}}$ is an ancillary ligand, have yielded some of the most promising results (Chart 11).250 Combinations of phenanthroline-based ligands $\left(\mathrm{I_{L}}\right)$ and 1,2- diaminocycloalkane ligands $\left(\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{L}}\right)$ gave impressive cytotoxicities against L1210 murine leukemia cells. Detailed SAR studies using different $^{R,R-}$ and $^{\sc s}$ -ancillary ligands revealed that chirality was a major determinant of toxicity.251 In terms of
Chart 11. Chemical Structures of Platinum(II) Complexes That Bind to DNA through Noncovalent Interactions
metallointercalators with diaminocyclopentane (DACP) ligands, $^{R,R}$ enantiomers are more potent than $^{\sc}{s},{s}$ enantiomers, whereas the reverse enantiomeric specificity was observed for metallointercalators with DACH ligands.247,251 The latter observation is in stark contrast to the activity of oxaliplatin, as described above.71 Within the PHENSS/RR series made up of 1,10-phenanthroline and trans-1,2-DACH ligands, the S,S enantiomer (PHENSS) exhibited 1 order of magnitude greater toxicity against leukemia cells than either the corresponding $^{R,R}$ enantiomer (PHENRR) or cisplatin. Given the encouraging biological activity of PHENSS, the compound was evaluated in prostate cancer xenograft mouse models. In vivo studies revealed that PHENSS was relatively nontoxic, and somewhat effective at reducing tumor growth over a period of 20 days as compared to saline controls.249 Unfortunately, statistically significant results were not obtained because the studies were carried out with a small number of mice. More detailed studies are needed to determine the complete in vivo potential of PHENSS. Recently, the 56MESS/RR series, made up of $^{5,6}$ - dimethyl-1,10-phenanthroline and trans-1,2-DACH ligands, has emerged as a highly promising anticancer candidate. Strikingly, the S,S enantiomer (56MESS) displays nanomolar toxicity toward leukemia cells. Although this compound interacts with DNA, the significance of DNA as an important cellular target has been questioned.255,256 Comprehensive mechanistic analysis of 56MESS in Madin Darby Canine Kidney (MDCK) cells revealed an increased expression of the mitochondria-associated protein labeled by MTC02, cell cycle arrest in synchronized and nonsynchronized cells, and caspase-independent cell death. Collectively, these observations suggest that the mechanism of cytotoxic action involves mitochondrial and cell cycle proteins rather than DNA.256
The cytotoxic potential of a planar platinum(II) complex bearing a tetradentate ligand, $\mathrm{(BDI^{\mathrm{\hat{Q}Q}})^{-}}$ , was reported recently.257 In aqueous buffer, the platinum $(\mathrm{II})$ complex $\mathrm{\big[Pt\big(BDI^{QQ}\big)\big]C l}$ (Chart 11) forms aggregates, but in the presence of DNA, the aggregates disperse yielding single molecules capable of intercalating between base pairs and unwinding DNA. $\mathrm{\big[Pt\big(BDI^{QQ}\big)\big]C l}$ exhibits selective toxicity for cancer cells over normal fibroblast cells and no cross-resistance with cisplatin in ovarian cancer cells. In a manner uncharacteristic of platinum(II) complexes, $\mathrm{\big[Pt\big(BDI^{QQ}\big)\big]C l}$ acts in a dual-threat manner.258 As well as attacking DNA in cells, which enriches p53 and BAX levels and subsequently induces mitochondria-mediated apoptosis, $\mathrm{\big[Pt\big(BDI^{QQ}\big)\big]\dot{C}l}$ also accumulates in mitochondria because of its lipophilic and cationic properties and causes direct mitochondrial damage.
The latter mechanism proceeds independently of $\mathsf{p}53$ and therefore allows $\mathrm{\big[Pt\big(BDI^{\hat{Q}Q}\big)\big]C l}$ to exhibit equal toxicity in p53- negative and p53-postive cells. Because $\mathsf{p}53$ activity is abrogated in many cancers,259 the $\mathsf{p}53$ -independence of $\mathrm{\big[Pt\big(BDI^{QQ}\big)\big]\mathrm{\overline{{C}}l}}$ is highly desirable in terms of preclinical development.
## 4.4. Monofunctional Platinum(II) Complexes
Mononuclear, monofunctional platinum(II) complexes represent another class of very propitious anticancer agents. These complexes contain only one labile ligand and are expected to form only one covalent bond to DNA. Unlike the complexes described above, which can form monofunctional adducts as well as bifunctional adducts, this class of compounds is designed to form at most one bond to DNA. Early studies revealed monofunctional platinum $(\mathrm{II})$ complexes such as $\mathrm{[Pt(NH_{3})_{3}C l]^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{[Pt(dien)Cl]^{+}}$ to be inactive both in vitro and in vivo.60,260 These results were in agreement with the prevailing viewpoint at the time, which stated that only neutral, square-planar platinum(II) complexes with a pair of inert ligands in a cis arrangement could have anticancer activity. This belief was overturned, in part, by work conducted by Engelhard Industries, which showed that monofunctional platinum(II) complexes of the form cis- $\mathrm{\small{[Pt(NH_{3})_{2}(A m)C l]^{+}}},$ where Am is an aromatic $N_{\mathbf{\ell}}$ -heterocyclic amine, inhibited tumor cell growth in vitro and in L1210 and P388 mouse leukemia models.261 Following this study, two platinum(II) complexes, cis-[Pt$\left(\mathrm{NH_{3}}\right)_{2}^{-}\left(9\mathrm{-aminoacridine}\right)\mathrm{Cl}]^{+}$ a n d c i s - $\left[\mathrm{Pt}.\right.$ $(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{chloroquine})\mathrm{Cl}]^{+}.$ , were prepared and shown to bind to DNA through both monofunctional covalent bond formation and intercalation.262 Unfortunately, murine sarcoma 180 ascites (S180a) screens revealed both complexes to have high dose-limiting toxicity $(5~\mathrm{{\mg}~\ k g^{-1})}$ , and any useful antineoplastic activity was masked by this systemic toxicity. The in vivo properties of these complexes were not investigated further.
Further studies with cis- $\mathrm{\Gamma}[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{Am})\mathrm{Cl}]^{+}$ complexes investigated the possibility that an ammine or Am group could be lost upon DNA binding, allowing bifunctional coordination.263 Careful analysis of NMR spectra and enzymatic digestion data on the products of the reaction of these complexes with $\mathsf{d}(\mathsf{G p G})$ and dG revealed the formation of monofunctional adducts only, with no evidence for the release of the ammine or Am groups.263 This result was supported by the fact that monoclonal antibodies capable of detecting bifunctional DNA lesions did not recognize cis$\bar{\mathrm{[Pt(NH_{3})_{2}(A m)C l]^{+}}}$ -induced DNA adducts. Electrophoretic mobility shift assays showed that the adducts formed by cis$\left[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{N}3\mathrm{-cytosine})\mathrm{Cl}\right]^{+}$ had minimal structural effects and that the DNA helix remained rod-like after treatment.264 Subsequent studies of the interaction of a structurally similar complex, cis- $\left[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}\right.$ (4-bromopyridine) $)\mathrm{Cl}]^{+}$ , with supercoiled DNA revealed that it bends and unwinds DNA less than traditional bifunctional complexes.265,266 A further vindication of the differing structural effects of monofunctional and bifunctional adducts was shown by the ability of HMGB proteins to recognize cisplatin modified DNA but not cis$\bar{\big[}\mathrm{Pt}\big(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{N}3\mathrm{-}\mathrm{cyfosine}\big)\bar{\mathrm{Cl}}\big]^{+}$ platinated DNA.267
Despite the aforementioned work on monofunctional platinum(II) complexes, no notable results of experiments with these compounds were reported for almost two decades, until a re-examination of these cationic monofunctional platinum(II) complexes arose unexpectedly from studies of the role of OCTs in the cellular uptake and activity of oxaliplatin.23,268 These studies uncovered the fact that cis$\mathsf{\widehat{\Gamma}}[\mathsf{P t}(\mathsf{\bar{N}H_{3}})_{2}(\mathsf{p y r i d i n e})\mathsf{C l}]^{+}$ (pyriplatin, Chart 12), a monofunctional, cationic platinum(II) compound, was well transported into cells that overexpressed organic cationic transporters 1 and 2.269 Cells with high OCT expression were more sensitive to pyriplatin treatment than those with low OCT expression. Moreover, the differential toxicity in pairs of cell lines with high versus low OCT expression was up to 137-fold for pyriplatin as compared to the equivalent toxicity ratio of up to 53-fold for oxaliplatin.269 Gel electrophoretic mobility shift assays showed that monofunctional DNA adducts formed by pyriplatin did not significantly unwind supercoiled DNA. Structural determination of a DNA dodecamer duplex site-specifically platinated with pyriplatin at the N7 site of a deoxyguanosine residue further revealed minimal perturbation to the structure of the BDNA double helix. In fact, hydrogen bonding between the platinated guanine base and its cytosine complement on the unplatinated strand was completely intact.269 In vitro studies in HOP-62 lung adenocarcinoma cells revealed that pyriplatin damages DNA, blocks cell cycle progression at the ${\bf G}2/{\bf M}$ phase, and prompts apoptotic cell death.270 Monofunctional lesions formed by pyriplatin are repaired by $\mathrm{NER},$ but not with the same fidelity as bifunctional lesions. NCI-60 cytotoxicity screening studies revealed that pyriplatin displays a novel spectrum of activity as compared to other platinum agents in this extensively employed database. The low potency of this compound relative to conventional platinum-based drugs, however, motivated a search for more active analogs. Elucidation of the X-ray crystal structure of RNA polymerase II stalled at a monofunctional pyriplatin-DNA lesion directed the strategy for making improvements.272 The structure suggested that larger $N.$ -heterocyclic ligands might more effectively block the progression of RNA polymerase II, leading to enhanced inhibition of transcription with consequential cytotoxicity. The pyridine in pyriplatin was therefore substituted by a small library of larger $N_{\mathbf{\ell}}$ -heterocycles. This process quickly led to the discovery of cis-[Pt$(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}$ (phenanthridine) $\lvert\mathrm{Cl}\rvert^{+}$ (phenanthriplatin, Chart 12) as having the desired improvement in efficacy. 273
According to the NCI-60 cytotoxicity screening assay, phenanthriplatin has a unique cancer cell-killing profile as compared to all other platinum agents held in the NCI archives.273 Unlike the potency of pyriplatin, that of phenanthriplatin toward cultured cancer cells is significantly greater than that of cisplatin. Upon loss of the chloride ligand, phenanthriplatin forms monofunctional adducts with derivatized guanine bases as well as duplex DNA. Phenanthriplatin− DNA adducts generate steric hindrance within the major groove and thus stall the progression of RNA polymerase II on DNA templates containing this damage. Kinetic studies of sitespecifically platinated DNA showed that the insertion of CTP opposite a platinated guanine by RNA polymerase II occurs in an error-free manner, but that mRNA elongation along the template DNA strand is halted.274 Phenanthriplatin−DNA adducts also inhibit DNA polymerases. DNA polymerase $\eta,$ a translesion synthase capable of bypassing 1,2-intrastand crosslinks formed by cisplatin, is able to insert the correct nucleotide opposite the phenanthriplatin-bound guanine, but cannot proceed further.275 A detailed mechanism of DNA polymerase $\eta$ inhibition was inferred from X-ray crystallographic data, which suggested that, despite the rotational freedom typically observed about $\mathrm{Pt}$ −amine bonds,276 the phenanthridine ring imposes a thermodynamic diastereoselectivity on the adduct that plays a significant role in blocking polymerase progression.275,277 S tudies with Escherichia coli resembling those conducted by Rosenberg further revealed that phenanthriplatin induces filamentous cell growth in a manner similar to that of cisplatin.278 Monofunctional platinum(II) complexes with little biological activity in cultured mammalian cancer cells were not able to replicate this result. Phenanthriplatin-mediated filamentous E. coli growth resulted from the bacterial SOS response, indicative of DNA damage. Thus far, data acquired for phenanthriplatin in cultured systems suggest that its anticancer activity, like that of cisplatin, is exerted through interaction with DNA, although the DNA binding preference is distinct from that of cisplatin.279
Platinum(II) complexes with tethered acridine units represent another important class of DNA-targeting anticancer agents. The first examples of such complexes consisted of acridine orange linked via trimethylene or hexamethylene tethers to (1,2-diaminoethane)dichloroplatinum(II) units capable of forming bifunctional DNA adducts.280−282 More recent versions contain a platinum moiety capable of forming monofunctional−DNA adducts and a planar acridine motif capable of intercalating between base pairs (Chart 12).283−285 In such cases, a semirigid linker is usually employed to promote platination of DNA bases directly adjacent to the intercalation site. An early example of this series, Pt-ACRAMTU, [PtCl(ethylenediamine)(ACRAMTU)] where ACRAMTU $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ 1-[2- (acridin-9-ylamino)ethyl]-1,3-dimethylthiourea, contains a chloride leaving group cis to an ACRAMTU group coordinated through a $\mathrm{Pt}{-}S$ bond.284 This arrangement was hypothesized to enable rapid DNA adduct formation without the need for rate-limiting aquation. The NMR spectroscopic solution structure of a site-specifically modified octamer containing a $\mathrm{Pt}$ -ACRAMTU adduct revealed that platination of a guanine within the major groove did not result in large structural changes. Intercalation of the appended acridine unit, however, did lengthen $(6.62\mathrm{~\AA})$ and unwind (twist, $15.4^{\circ}$ ) DNA.286 The authors propose that rapid intercalation precedes platination and that this mechanism is responsible for moving platinum away from DNA sites targeted by conventional platinum(II) agents. Clonogenic growth and cell proliferation studies showed that Pt-ACRAMTU and its derivatives were very active and display submicromolar $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ values against several cancer cell lines.287−292 Polymerase stop assays and flow cytometric assays demonstrated that the hybrid adducts inhibit RNA polymerase II and DNA synthesis.290,291,293 Inhibition of DNA synthesis led to S phase cell cycle arrest as opposed to ${\bf G}2/{\bf M}$ phase cell cycle arrest, as is customary following cisplatin treatment.293 Although Pt-ACRAMTU maintained submicromolar activity in cell lines with aberrant p53 and KRAS expression, preclinical development was halted because of their inability to stop tumor growth in corresponding xenograft mouse models.294 Other Pt-ACRAMTU analogs have been subsequently investigated. The most promising second generation derivative was developed by replacing the thiourea donor on Pt-ACRAMTU with an amidine .295,296 This modification accelerated DNA binding, increased cancer cell toxicity by 2 orders of magnitude (nanomolar $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ values), and inhibited tumor growth in vivo.295,296 Studies in NCI-H460 nonsmall cell lung cancer cells suggest that the impressive cytotoxicities can be attributed to rapid intracellular accumulation, DNA adduct formation, and less efficient repair of the DNA lesions. Unfortunately, the amidine-based complexes exhibit extremely high systemic toxicity in vivo, the maximum tolerated dose determined for one of the most potent complexes being $0.5~\mathrm{~mg~}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ . It is possible that the development of cancer cell-specific delivery agents could improve safety while maintaining potency.
A related class of compound has been reported in which a platinum complex is tethered to a rhodium complex capable of metalloinserting into DNA. Metalloinsertion is a noncovalent interaction distinct from metallointercalation in that the insertor, binding in the minor groove, ejects mismatched base-pairs out toward the major groove and assumes their place within the base-stack.297 Bimetallic Rh−Pt complexes are capable of targeting DNA through Rh-mediated metalloinsertion and Pt-mediated covalent cross-linking. Such bifunctional constructs have been made with linkage to bifunctional platinum centers via a nonleaving or leaving group ligand.298,299 Recently, such a construct featuring a monofunctional platinum center was prepared and reported to be toxic to both mismatch repair-deficient and mismatch repairproficient human colorectal carcinoma cells. In vitro mechanistic studies confirmed that this construct induces cell death via apoptosis rather than necrosis, as had been observed for other metalloinsertors.300
## 4.5. Other Platinum(II) Complexes
Bone and other calcified tissues show a great propensity to take up bisphosphonates, a property that has been exploited in the design of a number of drugs that target bone diseases.301−303 The ability of the bisphosphonate unit to chelate calcium confers this targeting ability. In an effort to develop cisplatin analogs that selectively target bone, a series of platinum(II) complexes were prepared with the chelating 2-amino $\mathrm{(m)}$ - ethylpyridine ligand functionalized at the amine with a bisphosphonate unit.304 In vitro cytotoxicity assays reveal that these complexes are less active than cisplatin. Flow cytometric studies suggest that the mechanism of cell death induced by the bisphosphonate complexes is different from that of cisplatin. The lack of observed DNA binding in cell-free circular dichroism and electrophoretic mobility studies supported the theory that an alternative cell killing mechanism was operative. It is possible to load platinum complexes bearing bisphosphonate leaving group ligands onto a solid matrix for implantation and release at the site of bone cancer. For further details, the reader is referred to a recent review of this area.305
A series of platinum complexes with pyrophosphate ligands in place of the traditional nonleaving group ligands afforded complexes that did not bind to DNA but in some cases were more active than the parent compound(s) from which they were derived.306 Evidence highlighting the distinctions between the biological effects of the classical platinum drugs and the pyrophosphate complexes has been accumulated, but no mechanistic model has yet been proposed.307
Platinum(II) complexes with diethyl[(methylsulfinyl)- methyl]phosphonate as the nonleaving group ligands have low cytotoxcity, but, in addition to DNA binding revealed by replication mapping experiments, these complexes also potently inhibit matrix metalloproteinases.308 Inhibition of matrix metalloproteinase 3 (MMP-3) was also observed with platinum complexes having three labile ligands. The authors provide evidence indicating that the platinum binds to a critical histidine residue in the enzyme.309 A subsequent crystal structure of the platinum bound matrix MMP-3 confirmed binding to this histidine.310
## 5. PLATINUM(IV) PRODRUGS THAT RELEASE CLASSICAL PLATINUM(II) ANTICANCER AGENTS
The anticancer potential of platinum(IV) agents has been recognized from the time of the original discovery of the biological properties of cisplatin,55 but their clinical value has only more recently been realized. The physicochemical properties of platinum(IV) agents differ significantly from those of their platinum(II) counterparts. Unlike square-planar platinum(II) complexes, platinum(IV) complexes are nearly always six-coordinate and adopt octahedral geometries (Figure 6). The saturated, kinetically much more inert coordination sphere of platinum(IV) is more resistant to ligand substitution reactions than four-coordinate platinum(II) centers, thus minimizing unwanted side reactions with biomolecules prior to DNA binding. Moreover, the two extra ligands afforded by low-spin $\mathsf{d}^{6}$ platinum(IV) centers provide a means to impart and fine-tune desired biological properties such as lipophilicity, redox stability, cancer-cell targeting, orthogonal or complementary bioactivity, and improved cellular uptake. The additional ligands also facilitate attachment to nanoparticles and other carrier systems, a detailed discussion of which is provided later in this Review.
Interpretation of the reactivity of platinum(IV) complexes with DNA and other biomolecules requires consideration of their rates of substitution and reduction. Although platinum(IV) complexes can platinate DNA in their oxidized form, the formation of cytotoxic lesions by ligand substitution occurs on the scale of weeks.312 Given that platinum agents are typically cleared from the body in a matter of hours, direct platinum(IV)−biomolecule coordination is unlikely to be of clinical importance.313 Reduction of the platinum(IV) center to platinum(II), in concert with the loss of two ligands, is thought to be essential for the anticancer activity of these agents. Specifically, the canonical mechanism of reduction involves the loss of the two axial ligands (Figure 6). The resulting divalent form, usually cisplatin or a related derivative, binds to DNA, inhibits transcription and replication, and induces apoptosis. The reduction process depends upon the composition of the platinum(IV) agent as well as the biological reducing agent involved. A convenient measure of reduction can be obtained from voltammetric experiments, but because the platinum(IV)- to-platinum(II) reduction is irreversible, a standard redox potential cannot be obtained as the midpoint potential from a typical cyclic voltammogram. Instead, the peak potential, $E_{\mathrm{{p}}},$ for the cathodic wave obtained in a cyclic voltammetric measurement is typically quoted. Care must be taken in interpreting these values, however, because the cathodic peak potential of an irreversible process is not dictated solely by the thermodynamics of reduction, but also by the heterogeneous charge transfer rate constant at the electrode surface and is influenced by the scan rate.314 The relative cathodic peak potentials of structurally related complexes are, however, typically correlated to the relative facility with which the metal center is reduced and have been correlated with reduction rates in solution.315 Early quantitative SAR studies showed that the nature of the axial ligands has a stronger influence on reduction rates than the equatorial ligands.316 Within the cis- $\left[{\mathrm{Pt}}(\mathbf{en})\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{X}_{2}\right]$ series where X is an anionic axial ligand, the chloride species $\left(E_{\mathrm{p}}=-4$ $\mathrm{mV},$ ) is much more susceptible to reduction than the carboxylate $\mathrm{mV})$ derivativeps.317,318 Systematic variation of equatorpial amine $\left(E_{\mathrm{p}}=-250{-}350\ \mathrm{mV}\right)$ or hydroxide ligands in complexes of general formula cis- $\big[\hat{\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}}\big(\mathrm{NHR}_{2}\big)_{2}\big],$ where $\mathbb{R}=\mathrm{alkyl}$ and aromatic hydrocarbons, did not have a considerable effect on the reduction rates.319 Platinum(IV) reduction by small biomolecules such as ascorbate and tgrlauntastfehri me chaan socmcsu.3r b−y3  nBerc- osre inutner--sphere ellecttrrontransfer requires the formation of a chemical bridge between the participating species, platinum(IV) agents with halide or hydroxide ligands trans to a good leaving group are particularly well suited to undergo reduction by this mechanism.322 Differences in ability to form such bridges can even override trends in reduction rate expected on the basis of differences in ligand-to-metal electron-donating ability.324 Bimolecular outersphere electron-transfer processes are generally slower than inner-sphere electron-transfers but can provide a viable reduction mechanism for platinum(IV) agents in which ligands that are capable of forming a bridge to an electron transfer agent are trans to firmly bound ligands.   
Ormaplatin, also known as tetraplatin or tetrachloro(trans
1,2-diaminocyclohexane)platinum(IV) (Chart 13), was one of the first platinum(IV) agents to undergo clinical trials. Ormaplatin is rapidly reduced to dichloro(trans-1,2- diaminocyclohexane)platinum(II) in tissue culture medium $\left(t_{1/2}=5{-}15\mathrm{min}\right)$ and undiluted rat plasma $\left(t_{1/2}=3~\mathrm{s}\right)$ ).325 The active platinum $(\mathrm{II})$ species is similar to oxaliplatin, but it contains both $^{R,R}$ and $^{\sc}$ isomers. Ormaplatin displayed in vitro and in vivo activity against some cisplatin-resistant cancers and was taken forward to clinical trials commissioned by NCI and Upjohn.326−330 Various doses, dose patterns, and modes of administration (intravenous and intraperitoneal) were investigated in six Phase I clinical trials, but no Phase II clinical trials have been announced.330−332 Ormaplatin induced severe neurotoxicity at the MTD, and in some cases a safe MTD could not be determined. Toxicity is thought to arise from fast reduction to the active platinum(II) form as a consequence of the axial chloride ligands.
Another notable platinum(IV) complex to have undergone clinical trials is iproplatin, also known as JM9, CHIP, or cis,trans,cis-dichlorodihydroxobis(isopropylamine)platinum(IV) (Chart 13).333 Iproplatin is structurally similar to ormaplatin in that it contains two equatorial chloride groups that are cis to one another. Carbon-14 labeling studies showed that the mechanism of action of iproplatin involves the reduction of the platinum(IV) center to platinum(II) followed by covalent bond formation with DNA.334 Iproplatin is less prone to reduction and deactivation by biological reducing agents than ormaplatin, presumably because of the presence of hydroxide axial ligands. The slower deactivation of this complex permits less hindered distribution throughout the body. Another advantage of iproplatin is its very high water solubility $\left(44.1~\mathrm{mM}\right)$ , which allows simpler formulation and administration. Iproplatin is one of the most clinically studied platinum agents to have not been approved for marketing, with 38 clinical trials ranging from Phase I to III having been concluded, many of which were commissioned by Johnson Matthey and Bristol Myers. Phase I studies revealed that the dose-limiting toxic effect was myelosuppression, which, in one study involving children, was partly correlated with the amount of prior chemo- and radiotherapy received.335 The same study recommended intravenous doses of $324~\mathrm{mg~m}^{-2}$ over $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ every 3−4 weeks for Phase II trials in children. Other studies proposed doses of $45{-}65$ and $95~\mathrm{mg~m}^{-2}$ for patients treated on a five-times daily schedule every 3 weeks and a four-times weekly schedule with two-week break periods, respectively.336 Phase II trials were carried out in patients with a variety of different cancer types.337−341 Phase III trials were conducted in ovarian cancer patients and those with metastatic epidermoid carcinoma of the head and neck.342,343 The ultimate conclusion of these studies was that iproplatin did not exhibit overall effectiveness that surpassed that of cisplatin or carboplatin, and no further trials were undertaken.
Satraplatin, trans,cis,cis-bis(acetato)amminecyclohexylaminedichloroplatinum(IV) (Chart 13), was the first platinum agent reported to have oral activity.344,345 Satraplatin was rationally designed such that the lipophilicity and stability were suitable for oral administration. The half-life of reduction of satraplatin by $\textit{s}_{\mathrm{mM}}$ ascorbate is $50\ \mathrm{min},$ which is adequate time for absorption by the gastrointestinal mucosa in the platinum(IV) form once ingested.346 Upon entry into the bloodstream, satraplatin undergoes reduction to give six distinct p l a t i n u m ( I I ) s p e c i e s . A m m i n e ( c y c l o h e x y l a m i n e ) - dichloroplatinum(II), derived from the loss of two acetate ligands, is the major metabolite and also exhibits the most potent anticancer activity.347,348 In preclinical studies, satraplatin exhibited a better toxicity profile than cisplatin and showed activity in cisplatin-resistant human tumor cell lines.344
Similarly to cisplatin, satraplatin acts through formation of DNA cross-links, DNA distortion, and subsequent inhibition of DNA transcription and replication. The ability of satraplatin to overcome cisplatin resistance is thought to arise from the asymmetric nature of the DNA lesions, which can evade recognition by DNA repair protein s.349−351 In vivo studies in mice bearing murine ADJ/PC6 plasmacytoma, which we note was the same model used to identify carboplatin as a viable alternative to cisplatin,352 showed satraplatin to exhibit markedly superior antitumor efficacy relative to cisplatin, carboplatin, and ormaplatin.344 Furthermore, in four ovarian carcinoma xenograft models of varying cisplatin and carboplatin resistance, satraplatin displayed activity similar to that of intravenously administered cisplatin or carboplatin and far superior to intraperitoneally administered ormaplatin. In rodents, the dose-limiting toxicity of satraplatin was myelosuppression. Encouragingly, less hepatotoxicity and fewer gastrointestinal effects were observed as compared to treatment with cisplatin or carboplatin. The favorable toxicity profile and preclinical antitumor activity of satraplatin prompted several development companies to begin Phase I clinical trials. In the first Phase I study, satraplatin was administered at doses ranging from 60 to $17\mathrm{{0}~m g~\bar{m}^{-2}}$ as a single oral dose.353,354 The pharmacokinetics data suggested that gastrointestinal absorption was being saturated, preventing the MTD from being reached. To improve absorption into the bloodstream, patients were treated on a five-times daily schedule with lower doses $\left(30-140\mathrm{~\mg~{~m}^{-2}}\right)$ .355 The dose-limiting toxicities were thrombocytopenia and neutropenia. In about $10\%$ of the patients treated, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea were also observed. On the basis of the Phase I studies, doses of 100−120 and $45{-}50~\mathrm{mg~m}^{-2}$ were recommended for repeated daily dosing for 5 and 14 days, respectively, in Phase II/III trials.356−358 A Phase II study on metastatic NSCLC patients, in which satraplatin was administered as single daily $120~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathbf{m}^{-2}$ doses for 5 days on 3 week cycles, failed to provide any objective responses.359 Nevertheless, $46\%$ of the patients were noted to express some palliation. A more advanced Phase II study on patients with small-cell lung cancer and squamous cell head and neck cancer, with escalated doses of satraplatin, produced a response rate of $38\%$ , similar to that observed with cisplatin.353 Encouragingly, this study found no signs of severe neurotoxicity or nephrotoxicity. Other Phase II studies in patients with relapsed ovarian cancer and advanced/recurrent squamous cancer of the cervix produced clinically beneficial or partial rates of response in several patients.360,361 The former study noted that the most common form of toxicity was neutropenia and thrombocytopenia. Satraplatin has also been heavily studied as a potential second-line chemotherapeutic for patients with metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer (CRPC).362,363 Treatment with $120\mathrm{\mg\m}^{-2}$ satraplatin daily for 5 days, used in patients with CRPC that had undergone front-line hormone therapy, resulted in $62\%$ of patients expressing stable disease or partial response. Follow-up Phase III trials commissioned by Bristol-Myers Squibb assessed the capability of satraplatin as a front-line chemotherapeutic in combination with prednisone.364 This study was terminated after only 50 of the intended cohort of 380 patients were treated because satraplatin in combination with prednisone was less efficacious than prednisone alone. Following this setback, satraplatin was acquired by GPC Biotech, who set up a multicenter, multinational, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase III trial called Satraplatin and Prednisone Against Refractory Cancer (SPARC) with 950 patients.365,366 The aim of the trial was to compare treatments of satraplatin and prednisone against placebo and prednisone, as second-line therapy for patients who had received a cytotoxic agent, which in some cases was docetaxel. The study found that the satraplatin/prednisone combinations led to a $36\%$ decrease in pain progression and an improvement in progression free survival rates. The positive outcome of the SPARC trial prompted GPC Biotech to file for FDA approval; however, this claim was rejected on the grounds that overall survival was not significantly improved and that more than one-half the patients in the study had received prior docetaxel treatment.367,368 A Phase I clinical trial has also been carried out with LA-12, a derivative of satraplatin in which the cyclohexylamine is replaced with adamantylamine (Chart 13).369
## 6. DUAL-THREAT PLATINUM(IV) PRODRUGS THAT RELEASE CLASSICAL PLATINUM(II) ANTICANCER AGENTS
In all of the examples discussed above, the platinum(IV) agent undergoes intracellular reduction to produce an active platinum(II) species and two biologically innocent groups. The inclusion of one or two biologically active ligands within the platinum(IV) scaffold can produce dual-threat platinum(IV) agents. The biologically active ligands are typically chosen to have non-DNA targets to limit cross-resistance with the DNA-targeting platinum(II) species released. There are several examples of dual-threat platinum(IV) anticancer agents in the literature; here we will discuss the most prominent examples.
Ethacraplatin comprises a cisplatin equatorial core axially coordinated to two ethacrynic acid ligands through their carboxylic acid groups (Chart 14).370,371 Ethacrynic acid and its glutathione adduct are potent inhibitors of glutathione-Stransferase (GST), an enzyme which aids the detoxification of platinum agents by catalyzing their conjugation to glutathione.372 Upon intracellular reduction, ethacraplatin releases cisplatin and 2 equiv of ethacrynic acid. Ethacrynic acid inhibits GST and reverses platinum drug resistance, enabling ethacraplatin to inhibit the growth of cisplatin-resistant breast, lung, and colon cancer cells more effectively than cisplatin alone. The platinum(IV) divalproate complex (VAAP) is another example of a dual-threat agent (Chart 14).373 Upon reduction, VAAP generates cisplatin and 2 equiv of valproic acid, a potent histone deacetylase (HDAC) inhibitor that stimulates differentiation and apoptosis in cancer cells. VAAP displays strong synergistic cytotoxicity, up to 2 orders of magnitude higher than cis,cis,trans-diamminedichlorodihydroxoplatinum(IV) alone or in combination with valproic acid. Furthermore, VAAP-loaded poly(ethylene glycol)-block-poly(caprolactone) nanoparticles (section 8) display pharmacologically relevant blood circulation times, with high tumor accumulation and significant growth inhibitory effects in lung adenocarcinoma xenograft mouse models. More recently, platinum(IV) derivatives of oxaliplatin with one or two valproate axial ligands have been prepared with the aim of developing VAAP derivatives with more favorable toxicity and tolerability profiles.374
In an effort to promote p53 activity and enhance cisplatin efficacy, a dual-threat construct in which a cisplatin core is attached to a chalcone derivative that can inhibit the interaction between p53 and MDM2, a negative inhibitor of p53, was recently prepared.375 The resulting complex, chalcoplatin (Chart 14), significantly enhanced p53 activation, which increased the rate of apoptosis resulting from the action of cisplatin released upon intracellular reduction. Chalcoplatin induced a pattern of cell cycle arrest distinct from that of cisplatin and similar to that of chalcone.
Cantharidin (exo-2,3-dimethyl-7-oxabicyclo[2.2.1]heptane2,3-dicarboxylic anhydride) is an inhibitor of protein phosphatase $2\mathrm{A},$ a protein that regulates oncogenic signaling cascades. Demethylcantharidin and endothall, the hydrolyzed, ring-opened derivative of demethylcantharidin, have fewer side effects than cantharidin but remain effective inhibitors of protein phosphatase 2A. Endothall was installed at the axial positions of platinum(IV) prodrugs by allowing demethylcantharidin to react with platinum(IV) complexes bearing nucleophilic axial hydroxide ligands (Chart 14).376 Prodrugs with cis-diamminedichloroplatinum or ethylenediaminedichloroplatinum cores were prepared, characterized, and evaluated using in vitro cytotoxicity assays. The ability of the endothall released upon reduction of the platinum(IV) center to carry out its biological function was not assessed in this work, but subsequent studies were performed using a related prodrug as described in section 8.
Texaphyrins are pentaaza macrocyclic molecules capable of forming complexes with a range of metal ions, including lanthanides.377 To capitalize on the tumor-targeting capabilities that some texaphyrins exhibit, platinum(II) complexes were tethered to the macrocycle; however, these initial complexes demonstrated poor aqueous stability.378,379 To improve upon this feature, kinetically inert cisplatin prodrugs were prepared in which a gadolinium-texaphyrin complex was conjugated to the platinum center through an axial succinate ligand.380 The platinum(IV) constructs did indeed demonstrate enhanced aqueous stability, but were sensitive to light, undergoing photoreduction upon exposure to glass-filtered sunlight. This mode of reduction might render the compounds useful for phototherapy in a manner similar to photoactivatable platinum(IV) azide complexes (section 7), platinum(IV) upconversion nanoparticle constructs (section 8), and quantum dot constructs (section 8).
Cyclooxygenases (COX) are enzymes that catalyze key steps in prostaglandin biosynthesis. Prostaglandins are lipid molecules involved in inflammation and have also been implicated in tumorigenesis.381 A range of COX-inhibitors have been developed including the widely used nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). The first platinum(IV) prodrug conjugate of an NSAID was independently reported by two groups. The molecule, known as either asplatin or platin-A (Chart 14), has a cisplatin core and axial acetylsalicylate ligands.382,383 Shortly thereafter, prodrugs capable of releasing either cisplatin or oxaliplatin along with indomethacin or ibuprofen were described.384,385 The activity of these prodrugs was attributed to the lipophilicity afforded by the axial ligands rather than COX inhibition stemming from their release.
Normal cells generate a significant proportion of their required energy within the mitochondria through the action of the citric acid cycle and oxidative phosphorylation. Under the hypoxic conditions present in many tumor tissues, however, cancer cells obtain a larger proportion of their energy by aerobic glycolysis. This phenomenon is known as the Warburg effect.80 The difference in metabolic pathways operational in normal and cancer cells has been exploited by mitaplatin, a platinum(IV) complex designed to selectively kill cancer cells over nonmalignant cells (Chart 14).386 Mitaplatin consists of two dichloroacetate (DCA) ligands appended to a cisplatin core. Upon reduction in cancer cells, DCA inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase kinase (PDK), which in turn reduces the flux of metabolites through aerobic glycolysis and restores normal mitochondrial function. This process promotes apoptosis by releasing cytochrome c from mitochondria and translocating apoptosis-inducing factor (AIF) to the nucleus. At the same time, free cisplatin induces DNA damage in the usual manner and prompts apoptotic cell death. The concerted action of cisplatin and DCA allows mitaplatin to kill lung carcinoma cells (A549) more readily than normal lung fibroblasts (MRC5) in cocultured in vitro systems. Subsequent work showed that this mechanism of action is able to overcome cisplatin resistance in human epidermoid adenocarcinoma and hepatoma cancer cells.387 Detailed biophysical studies investigating the aqueous chemistry of mitaplatin and related platinum(IV) complexes with axial haloacetate ligands revealed that, contrary to the typical dogma that platinum(IV) prodrugs are inert to ligand substitution, the axial ligands of these complexes can be substituted for hydroxide under biologically relevant conditions.388 Isotopic labeling studies revealed that the hydrolysis proceeds via the attack of a hydroxide ion on the platinum(IV) center, and not at the carbonyl of the haloacetate. At physiological $\mathrm{\boldmath~\pH,}$ however, the half-life of hydrolysis is much longer than the rate at which mitaplatin is cleared from the blood of rodents,389 and so this process most likely has little influence on the in vivo effects of this compound.
One of the major disadvantages of mitaplatin is the widely differing effective doses of the reduced products, DCA and cisplatin, which alone exhibit their characteristic activities at millimolar and micromolar concentrations, respectively. Although incorporation of anionic DCA into neutral mitaplatin could increase the efficacy of DCA by increasing cellular uptake, the large disparity in activity prompted the search for a more potent secondary component. To achieve a better match in activity between the intracellular reduction products, DCA was replaced with a vitamin E analog, $\alpha$ -tocopheryl succinate ( $\scriptstyle{\dot{\alpha}}-$ TOS).390 $\alpha$ -TOS inhibits the antiapoptotic proteins Bcl-2 and Bcl-xL in the micromolar range, thereby inducing mitochondria-mediated apoptotic cell death at comparable concentrations to cisplatin. Platinum(IV) complexes comprising cisplatin attached to one or two $\alpha$ -TOS ligands were prepared. The disubstituted derivative was nontoxic, presumably because of its high lipophilicity and susceptibility to entrapment inside the cell membrane. In contrast, the monosubstituted derivative (Chart 15A) exhibited cytotoxicity 7−25 times greater than that of cisplatin across several tumor cell lines. Mechanistic studies revealed that this complex induces nuclear DNA damage and simultaneous mitochondrial membrane depolarization because of inhibition of Bcl-xL-Bax protein−protein interactions.
In $\mathrm{ER}+$ breast cancer cells, addition of estrogen increases HMGB1 expression. HMGB1 inhibits repair of cisplatininduced DNA lesions by preventing DNA repair proteins from accessing the site of damage.391 Thus, cotreatment with unconjugated estrogen and cisplatin enhances the activity of cisplatin.392 Incorporation of two axially coordinated estrogen moieties into a cisplatin prodrug enabled the preparation of cisplatin−estrogen conjugates capable of concurrently releasing both groups (Chart 15B).393 Given that the estrogen units were modified with ester groups, hydrolysis to generate free estradiol is a prerequisite for activity. $\mathrm{ER}+$ MCF-7 cells treated with the platinum(IV)−estrogen complex displayed an increase in HMGB1 expression to a degree similar to that observed following treatment with estradiol. The activity of estradiol potentiates the activity of cisplatin. As predicted on the basis of the construct design, HMGB1 levels in ER− HCC-1937 cells were less affected, and the $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ values were comparatively higher (up to 1.8-fold).
Platinum(IV) agents with axially coordinated bioactive peptides have been designed and prepared with the aim of targeted drug delivery to tumors. Several mono- and difunctionalized platinum(IV) complexes with tri- and pentapetides capable of binding to $\alpha_{\mathrm{V}}\beta_{3}$ and $\alpha_{\mathrm{V}}\beta_{5}$ integrins and aminopeptidase N (APN) on the surface of cancer cells have been prepared.394 Integrins and APN are highly expressed in tumor-induced angiogenesis, and thus the platinum(IV) conjugates are able to selectively target angiogenic tumor cells over primary proliferating endothelial cells. The antiproliferative effect of the platinum(IV)−peptide conjugates decreased upon coincubation with $\alpha_{\mathrm{V}}\beta_{3^{-}}$ and $\alpha_{\mathrm{V}}\beta_{5}$ -specific peptides and transfection with $\beta_{3}$ integrin siRNA, confirming that their activity was mediated by the recognition of specific integrins on the cancer cell membrane surface. A very recent report describes the enhancement that can be obtained by using an axial ligand that displays multiple units of the cyclic c(RGDfK) integrin-targeting peptide.395 In this work, a picoplatin prodrug displaying a tetrameric c(RDGfK) motif was able to accumulate in cells overexpressing $\alpha_{\mathrm{V}}\beta_{3}$ and $\alpha_{\mathrm{V}}\beta_{5}$ integrins resulting in a 10-fold enhancement in cytotoxicity over cells that do not express these membrane proteins. A cyclic RGD motif was also used in the construction of a theranostic platinum(IV) complex capable of targeting $\alpha_{\mathrm{V}}\beta_{3}$ integrins, releasing cisplatin upon reduction, and reporting on the activation of apoptosis using an aggregation-induced emission fluorophore conjugated to a caspase-3 sensitive Asp-Glu-Val-Asp (DEVD) peptide.396 The two different peptides were attached to the two axial positions of a cisplatin prodrug.
Platinum(IV) complexes with analogs of neurotensin and somatostatin have also been reported with the aim of targeting cancer cell lines expressing the corresponding receptors.397 Cytotoxicity studies revealed that, although cell-killing was improved as compared to the nontargeting platinum(IV) precursor, cellular uptake was nonspecific, presumably because the receptors were unable to recognize the peptides once tethered to platinum(IV). Fusion of cell penetrating peptides (CPPs), such as a TAT (Trans-acting Activator of Transcription) protein fragment, with platinum(IV) centers has yielded very active conjugates. The oxaliplatin-TAT monoconjugate displays 39-fold higher cytotoxicity against cultured cancer cells than the corresponding platinum(IV) analog with no targeting peptide(s).398 Large peptides like chlorotoxin (CTX) have also been attached to platinum(IV) in a 1:1 ratio for targeting purposes.399 CTX, a 36-amino-acid peptide found in the venom of the deathstalker scorpion ,400 binds to functional proteins like matrix metalloproteinase 2 (MMP2), annexin A2, and chloride ion channels, which are overexpressed on certain cancer cell surfaces. The platinum(IV)−chlorotoxin conjugate exhibits higher in vitro toxicity against human cervical HeLa cells than the nontargeted platinum(IV) building block. The difference in cytotoxicity is attributed to the targeting of annexin A2 and chloride ion channels present on the cell surface of HeLa cells.
## 7. PLATINUM(IV) COMPLEXES WITH NONCISPLATIN-LIKE MECHANISMS OF ACTION
Before it became established that most anticancer-active platinum(IV) complexes function as prodrugs that undergo reduction to release active platinum(II) agents, alternative mechanisms of action for platinum(IV) complexes were envisioned. Because of the steric restraints imposed by the DNA double helix, it seemed unlikely that DNA cross-linking of the cisplatin type was operative,15 although modeling studies suggest that such adducts might be able to form.401 Monofunctional adducts could also conceivably form,402,403 but the generally slow kinetics of ligand substitution at platinum(IV) argue against this process being of great biological relevance. A series of studies have appeared, however, describing the platinum(IV)-mediated oxidation of guanine to 8-oxo-guanine. The first report of this reactivity came from the observation that incubation of $\mathrm{Pt(DACH)Cl_{4}}$ with GMP or dGMP led to the formation of $\mathrm{Pt}\big(\mathrm{DACH}\big)\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ .404 The oxidation of guanine was confirmed as was the ability of the oxidation to occur with guanine bases in double-stranded oligonucleotides. The reactions typically occurred on the order of days, and theoretical calculations suggest that the reaction proceeds via a cyclic $_{5^{\prime},8}$ -phosphodiester intermediate.405,406 Analysis of kinetic data revealed that platinum $(\mathrm{II})$ centers can catalyze the reaction, which is initiated by intermolecular nucleophilic attack, for example, by phosphate.407,408 Another form of platinum(IV)-mediated DNA damage was reported following the observation that cis,cis,trans- $[\bar{\mathrm{Pt}}(\mathrm{NH_{3}})_{2}\bar{\mathrm{Cl}}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}]$ could cleave double-stranded DNA, but this reactivity was subsequently shown to arise from molecules of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ that cocrystallized with the platinum(IV) complex.409,410
Photoactivated chemotherapy offers temporal and spatial control over drug activation and has shown potential for the treatment of several cancers including those of the skin, lung, brain, and esophagus. The activated toxic species is produced by irradiation only where it is required, allowing tumors to be targeted specifically. This approach is advantageous over other therapies such as surgery, radiotherapy, and conventional chemotherapy because, ideally, normal tissue is not affected and the treatment can be repeated as often as required. In an attempt to increase selectivity and lower systemic toxicity of platinum agents, photoactivatable platinum(IV) prodrugs have been developed.411,412 Two main classes of photoactivatable platinum(IV) have been reported in the literature thus far, diiodo- and diazido-platinum(IV) complexes.
The first generation of photoactivatable platinum(IV) complexes was based on iodide as the photoactivating ligand and ethylenediamine as the nonleaving group (Chart 16).413,414 A bidentate ligand was chosen to prevent photoinduced isomerization, which could lead to the formation of thermodynamically stable but potentially inactive trans
## Chart 16. Chemical Structures of Photoactivatable Platinum $(\mathbf{II})$ −Diiodo Complexes
congeners. cis-Diiodoplatinum(IV) complexes exhibit dissociative LMCT/d−d excited states that can be populated by excitation with visible light. It was postulated that population of these states could trigger photoreduction and photosubstitution at the platinum(IV) center. The resulting platinum(II) species were then expected to coordinate to DNA bases and induce apoptosis. The first diiodo-platinum(IV) complex reported was prepared with axial chloride ligands.413 Although this complex was able to irreversibly platinate DNA upon irradiation at 375 nm, a similar effect was also observed in the dark, probably because of facile reduction due to the poor ability of chloride ligands to stabilize the $^{4+}$ oxidation state of the metal. To prevent chemical reduction in the dark, hydroxide-, acetate-, and methylsulfonate-based ligands were introduced at the axial position.414 As anticipated, the modified platinum(IV) complexes had better dark-stability, the methylsulfonate complex being the most stable with a half-life of $\dot{7}2\mathrm{~h~}$ Upon irradiation at $375~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ , the complexes underwent photoreduction, giving platinum species with differing DNA platination propensities. Photolysis of the platinum(IV) acetate complex generated the highest number of platinum−DNA adducts. 1D- and 2D-NMR spectroscopic experiments indicated that photoreduction to the corresponding platinum(II) species was necessary for DNA binding.415 In vitro studies with TCCSUP bladder cancer cells with and without $1.5\mathrm{~h~}$ of irradiation showed that the photolysis products were more cytotoxic than the parent platinum(IV) complexes, but the toxicity differential was not as high as expected.413,414 A statistically significant difference between dark and light $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ values was only observed for the platinum(IV)−acetate complex. To understand the underlying reasons for the high toxicity of the diiodo platinum(IV) complexes in the dark, biophysical studies were conducted with sulfur-rich biomolecules.416 NMR spectroscopic analysis showed that glutathione and N-acetylcysteine rapidly reduced the complexes to the reactive platinum(II) form by an inner-sphere mechanism similar to ormaplatin reduction. Given their fast reduction rates in the presence of biologically relevant thiols, diiodo− platinum(IV) complexes were deemed unsuitable for development as photoactivatable drugs.
Because several transition metal azide complexes are lightsensitive and undergo photosubstitution and photoreduction reactions, the iodide ligands in the aforementioned complexes were substituted for azide ligands to prepare a second generation of photoactivatable platinum(IV) prodrugs. The earliest example was trans- $\mathrm{[Pt(\bar{N}_{3})_{2}(C N)_{4}]^{2-}}$ , which, upon irradiation into the $302~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ LMCT band, led to trans elimination of the azide ligands by azidyl radical formation and reduction to platinum(II).417 The two radicals can rapidly decompose in aqueous solution to produce molecular nitrogen. This process prevents reoxidation of the platinum center, unlike halogen-based radicals that do not decompose in water and instead interact with the metal center to regenerate the starting material. Early biologically active platinum(IV)−diazide complexes were prepared with the azide ligands disposed cis to one another, trans to ammine or ethylenediamine nonleaving groups, and cis to axially coordinated hydroxide ligands (Chart 17).418 Unlike the diiodo−platinum(IV) complexes, the azide-bearing complexes were stable toward hydrolysis for up to 90 days and did not react readily with glutathione, in the dark, over the course of several weeks. Sophisticated NMR spectroscopic and biophysical experiments showed that photoreduction was dependent on the nonleaving groups present and the wavelength of light used for irradiation.419,420 Irradiation with blue and UV light triggered the formation of many platinum(II) and platinum(IV) species, indicating that photoreduction was not the sole photochemical process taking place. Indeed, in this system, irradiation is thought to promote photosubstitution, photoisomerization, and photoaquation. Cytotoxicity studies with the 5637 human bladder cancer cell line showed that irradiation significantly enhanced the potency of the complexes, from ${>}300$ to ca. $50~\dot{\mu}\mathrm{M}$ .419 Experiments with cisplatin-resistant 5637 cells indicated that the diazido− platinum(IV) complexes displayed no cross-resistance with cisplatin. Although in vitro assays indicate that the diazido− platinum(IV) complexes can bind DNA upon irradiation and inhibit RNA synthesis,421 fluorescence microscopy studies showed none of the typical signs of apoptosis. This finding implies that the irradiated diazido−platinum(IV) complexes might induce cell death in a manner different from that of cisplatin.
Diazido−platinum(IV) complexes containing azide ligands in a trans arrangement displayed more favorable electronic properties than their cis congeners (Chart 17).420,422 For instance, within the $\left[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{N}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}\right]$ series, the LMCT band was shifted toward the visible region for the trans isomer, allowing activation with tissue-penetrating light. Also, trans,- trans,trans- $\left[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{N}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)_{2}]$ is stable under biological conditions and, upon irradiation with red light, binds readily to DNA bases and induces toxicity in human HaCaT keratinocytes to a degree similar to that of cisplatin. Impressive phototoxicity was also observed against cisplatin-resistant cell lines. More recently, diazido−platinum(IV) complexes with higher photocytotoxicity have been prepared by replacing one or two $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ ligands with pyridine, methylamine, or thiazole (Chart 17).423−425 These complexes are resistant to hydrolysis and reduction in the dark and only become active upon irradiation with UVA or blue light. The photolysis products are highly toxic toward cancer cells and display no cross-resistance with cisplatin in ovarian carcinoma cells. Toxicity is attributed to the formation of a novel combination of mono- and bifunctional DNA adducts, primarily with guanine and cytosine, that unwind DNA. Detailed biophysical studies showed that the trans,trans,trans- $\bar{\big[}\mathrm{Pt}\big(\mathrm{N}_{3}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{OH}\big)_{2}^{-}$ (methylamine)(pyridine)] complex induced oxidation of guanine upon irradiation.426 This unexpected result is thought to arise from the reaction of singlet oxygen and platinum-nitrene intermediates. Extensive fluorescence experiments ruled out singlet oxygen generation from dissolve dioxygen or water. The most plausible source of singlet oxygen is the axially coordinated hydroxide groups. Guanine oxidation is a form of mutagenic DNA damage, and so this process could be a contributing factor in the mechanism of action of trans-diazido-platinum(IV) complexes. Because this singlet oxygen generation and subsequent guanine oxidation do not require any exogenous source of oxygen, it could be applied to target cancer cells that reside in hypoxic niches.
## 8. NANODELIVERY OF PLATINUM(IV) COMPLEXES
As described above, one prominent paradigm in the design of platinum(IV) anticancer agents is that of a prodrug bearing equatorial ligands identical to those of a platinum $\mathrm{(II)}$ complex
Chart 18. Carbon-Based Delivery Systems for Platinum(IV) Prodrugs Including Single-Walled Carbon Nanotubes (SWCNTs), Multi-Walled Carbon Nanotubes (MWCNTs), and Spherical Carbon Nanoparticles
with established anticancer activity and axial ligands chosen to either modulate the physicochemical properties of the compound or confer additional biological activity. The main impetus for altering the physicochemical properties of the complex is the attendant change that occurs in pharmacological activity. An alternative motive that guides the development of some platinum(IV) prodrugs is the desire to incorporate the complex into a drug delivery device, particularly those with nanoscale dimensions.
Nanodelivery of biologically active agents is a blossoming field at the intersection of materials science, engineering, medicine, and chemistry. The advantages to be gained from any drug delivery system, macroscopic or nanoscale, include the ability to reduce the systemic dose but increase the amount of active agent that reaches the target site. Broadly defined, nanoscale drug delivery is the use of any object with dimensions in the nanometer regime to transport pharmaceutically active agents. Nanoparticles can often be engineered to have sustained circulation, affording any cargo that they transport an enhanced retention in the bloodstream.427 In the nanodelivery of anticancer agents, the main advantages of using nanoparticles relate to their ability to target tumor tissue in either an active or a passive manner.428,429 Active targeting can be realized in a manner similar to that described above for small molecules. If the surface of the nanoparticle is decorated with a ligand for a receptor expressed selectively on the surface of cancer cells, then the particle is more likely to be taken up by those cells via receptor-mediated endocytosis.430 Multivalent effects that arise from the presence of multiple targeting units on the surface of a nanoparticle can also enhance this mode of uptake.431 Passive targeting arises directly from the ability of nanoscale objects in sustained circulation to accumulate in tumor tissue over time. This phenomenon, known as the enhanced permeation and retention (EPR) effect, occurs because the tumor vasculature is inherently leaky and the tumor tissue is poorly irrigated by the lymphatic system (Figure 7).432 As a result, nanoparticles with dimensions in the 50−200 nm range can readily extravasate into the tumor interstitial space (permeation) and remain there (retention), releasing their contents into the extracellular space of the tumor microenvironment or being taken up by cancer cells.
One broad strategy in the nanodelivery of platinum anticancer agents involves the use of platinum(IV) synthons similar to those employed to conjugate platinum(IV) centers to peptides or bioactive small molecules, as described above. In particular, cisplatin prodrugs with axial succinate ligands have enabled the functionalization of a variety of nanoscale objects using simple ester- and amide-bond forming reactions. A range of different platinum(IV) complexes and nanomaterials have been used for this purpose.427,431,434 The following is a review of the systems that have appeared in the peer-reviewed literature, grouped according to the nanomaterial that is used as the delivery vector.
## 8.1. Carbon-Based Materials
Carbon nanotubes have been extensively investigated as drug delivery vehicles, and a number of platinum(IV) prodrugcontaining constructs have been prepared.435 One early example of such platinum(IV) prodrug delivery was the use of single walled carbon nanotubes (SWCNTs) to ferry a cytotoxic platinum payload into cancer cells.436 The SWCNTs were rendered biocompatible and water dispersible with noncovalently binding phospholipid-PEG-amine. The phospholipid interacted with the nanotube surface, the PEG chain acted as a spacer, and the pendent amine functional group provided a reactive handle through which to couple the pendent carboxylic acid of cis,cis,trans- $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{P t}(\mathrm{NH_{3}})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\mathsf{\Gamma}($ OEt) $\left(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CCH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H}\right)]$ (Chart 18). An average of 65 platinum centers could be conjugated to each SWCNT. Fluorescence microscopy confirmed that cultured testicular cancer cells take up the SWCNTs conjugated to both the platinum(IV) prodrug and a fluorescent reporter molecule, and trap the nanotubes within endosomes. The platinum is then released into the rest of the cell.
Subsequent studies with this nanotube system used the disuccinate complex, cis,cis,trans- $\mathrm{\small{~\left[Pt(NH_{3})_{2}C l_{2}(O_{2}C C H_{2}\cdot}}$ - $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H})_{2}]$ , to allow conjugation to not only the functionalized carbon nanotube, but also to a targeting unit, folic acid (Chart 18).437 A number of human cancer cells (including ovarian, breast, lung, kidney, and colon cancer cells) overexpress the folate receptor. Immunohistochemical methods were used to establish that ${>}90\%$ of ovarian cancers overexpress the folate receptor,438 a result more recently confirmed using a quantitative radioligand binding assay.439 Inclusion of the targeting unit selectively directed the platinum-bearing SWCNT longboats, as the SWCNTs were dubbed, to $\mathrm{FR}+$ human choriocarcinoma (JAR) and nasopharyngeal carcinoma (KB) cells, but not to FR− human testicular carcinoma cells, which typically display marked sensitivity to cisplatin.437
In addition to conjugation to the nanotube surface, the internal cavities of these structures provide attractive opportunities for drug delivery. Multiwalled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs), which typically have larger internal diameters than SWCNTs, were loaded with the hydrophobic cisplatin prodrug cis,cis,trans- $[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{NH_{3}})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CC}_{6}\dot{\mathrm{H}}_{5})_{2}\hat{]}$ by nanoextraction over a period of several days (Chart 18).440,441 After extensive washing, the surface of the nanotubes bore no platinum, as confirmed by energy dispersive $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray spectroscopy, but the tubes had been loaded with the platinum complex to a degree quantifiable by atomic emission spectroscopy. The construct did not release any platinum unless a reducing agent was present. Ascorbic acid, for instance, can reduce the prodrug, reversing its hydrophobicity, and allowing release of cisplatin. The activity of this construct was further enhanced by functionalizing the surface of the nanoparticle with a fluorescent rhodamine dye prior to loading with the prodrug.442 This dye served as a targeting agent, directing the nanotubes to mitochondria. Although unloaded rhodamine-functionalized multiwalled nanotubes had little effect on the viability of cultured cells and did not appear to disrupt mitochondrial function, coencapsulation of the cisplatin prodrug bearing axial benzoate ligands and 3-bromopyruvate, a compound used to perturb the altered metabolism of cancer cells,443 afforded enhanced anticancer activity in vitro. A decrease in the mitochondrial membrane potential was observed. In vivo studies of the biodistribution of the platinum-loaded multiwalled nanotubes without any surface modification revealed that, in mice, the platinum(IV)-loaded construct exhibited decreased levels of platinum in the liver and kidney as compared to treatment with cisplatin.444 Accumulation in the lungs, however, was increased. Analysis of histological slices and cytokine levels indicates that no inflammation or abnormal immune response occurred.
As mentioned above, multiwalled nanotubes were chosen for these encapsulation-based constructs because they typically have larger internal cavities than SWCNTs. The dependence of platinum(IV) prodrug release on the diameter of the MWCNTs was recently probed.445 Smaller MWCNTs, once loaded with the prodrug, release platinum more slowly, as expected given the smaller size of the opening through which the complex must diffuse to escape. In addition to delivering a prodrug that only releases cisplatin, dual-threat prodrugs can also be loaded into MWCNTs. A prodrug was designed that is capable of releasing 1 equiv of doxorubicin for every equivalent of cisplatin released (Chart 18).446 This feat was achieved by generating an amide bond between the amine group of doxorubicin and the succinate of cis,cis,trans-[Pt$(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CC}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5})$ $(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CCH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H})$ ]. The final complex is sufficiently hydrophobic to be encapsulated within the nanotubes and, importantly, releases the two chemotherapeutic agents at relative concentrations close to those administered during combination chemotherapy. The integrin-targeting peptide, c(RGDfK), was used to functionalize the nanotubes to provide an active targeting mechanism. One potential complication observed by the authors is that reduction of the platinum(IV) center does not release doxorubicin, but rather the succinyl amide derivative of the drug, which alters its subcellular distribution.
Carbon nanoparticles are a recent addition to the repertoire of carbon-based nanoscale objects. This fluorescent material was first isolated as a side product in the arc-discharge synthesis of SWCNTs,447 but subsequent studies have led to its production by methods varying from the hydrothermal treatment of orange juice448 to nitric acid digestion of candle soot.449 Upon surface passivation, these materials exhibit striking photophysical properties,450 and they have been exploited for a variety of biological applications.451 Very recently, the photoactivatable platinum(IV) azide complex cis,trans,cis- $\mathrm{\overline{{{[Pt(N_{3})_{2}(O H)_{2}(N H_{3})\bar{(3\cdot N H_{2}p y)]}}}}}$ was conjugated to a carboxylate-functionalized carbon nanoparticle (Chart 18).452 Folic acid was also conjugated to the particle through an ethylenediamine linker. These surface modifications did not alter the structure of the nanoparticles, as revealed by transmission electron microscopy, and photoelectron spectroscopy confirmed the elemental composition of the construct. UV irradiation led to photoreduction of the platinum(IV) species, which was proposed to arise not just from direct population of the excited state of the platinum complex, but also by photoinduced electron transfer from the carbon nanoparticles. In vitro studies exploited the inherent luminescence of these nanoparticles to monitor preferential cellular uptake by $\mathrm{FR}+$ cells. Cytotoxicity assays confirmed the capacity of this construct to kill cultured cancer cells upon irradiation with UV light. A related carbon-based nanoparticle delivery system comprises PEGylated nanosized graphene oxide conjugated to cis,cis,trans- $\mathrm{\bar{[Pt(NH_{3})_{2}C l_{2}(O H)\bar{(O_{2}C C H_{2}-}}}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H})\mathrm{}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ] and an apoptosis sensing peptide.453 One motivation for using nanosized graphene oxide as the delivery platform was the established ability of this material to absorb near-IR light and release the energy as heat, a property that has been explored for photothermal therapy applications.454,455 In vivo studies involving a murine breast cancer xenograft confirmed that near-IR irradiation of tumors following intravenous administration of the construct completely inhibited tumor growth.
## 8.2. Gold Nanoparticles
Gold nanoparticles provide another nanodelivery platform to which platinum(IV) prodrugs have been covalently conjugated. The pendent carboxylate of a platinum(IV)−succinate complex similar to those described above can be conjugated to gold nanoparticles that are functionalized with thiolated, dodecylamine-terminated 28-mer oligonucleotides.456 The specific platinum complex delivered was ci s,cis,trans -[P t(NH3)2Cl2(OH) $(\mathrm{\bar{O}_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H)}_{\mathrm{\bar{\cdot}}}^{-}$ ] (Chart 19A), and the characteristic surface plasmon visible absorption band of the nanoparticles was used to confirm that they remained dispersed and did not aggregate. Fluorescence microscopy was used in conjunction with fluorescently labeled nanoparticles to monitor the progression of the constructs from vesicles to the cytosol. The R-C18 antibody, which was raised against the $1,2\mathrm{-d}\dot{(}\mathrm{GpG)}$ intrastrand DNA cross-link,457 was then used to detect the formation of this cytotoxic adduct, confirming that the platinum released from the nanoparticle construct is able to induce the same DNA lesion as cisplatin.
Another system employing gold nanoparticles involved the use of a cisplatin prodrug functionalized with an axial ligand bearing a terminal adamantyl unit capable of interacting with $\beta\mathrm{.}$ - cyclodextrin. Using this characteristic noncovalent interaction, cis,cis,trans- $\mathrm{\cdot[Pt(N\check{H}_{3})_{2}C l_{2}(O H)(O_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C(O)N H C H_{2}}+$ $\left(\mathrm{C}_{10}\mathrm{H}_{14}\right)]$ (Chart 19C) was loaded onto gold nanoparticles that had been surface-functionalized with a thiolate modified $\beta\mathrm{.}$ - cyclodextrin.458 The host−guest interaction was studied in solution using NMR spectroscopy, but in vitro cytotoxicity studies revealed that the nanoconstruct was less active than cisplatin itself, perhaps reflecting an inhibition of the platinum(IV) reduction event that is required for cytotoxicity.
Glutathione-stabilized gold nanoparticles were used to prepare a platinum-bearing, targeted drug delivery system. The use of glutathione as the surface passivating agent of the nanoparticles allowed for conjugation to the pendent reactive groups of this tripeptide. The cisplatin prodrug cis,cis,trans$\mathrm{\bar{\big[}P t\big(\bar{N}H_{3}\big)_{2}C l_{2}\big(O_{2}\bar{C}\bar{C}H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H\big)_{2}\big]}$ (Chart 19B) was conjugated to the surface, as was the neuropilin-1 receptortargeting peptide, CRGDK. In vitro studies confirmed that delivery was enhanced in cells that express high levels of the neuropilin-1 receptor as compared to those that express low levels of it.
Gold nanorods, particles with one dimension significantly longer than those of gold nanoparticles, have also been investigated for their drug delivery capabilities.459 The cisplatin prodrug cis,cis,trans- $[\mathrm{Pt}(\bar{\mathrm{NH}}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CCH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{C}\bar{\mathrm{O}}_{2}\mathrm{H})]$ (Chart 19A) was conjugated to the surface of PEGylated gold nanorods, the PEG chains of which were terminally modified with amine groups.460 The conjugation afforded a stable construct, as determined by probing the surface plasmon electronic absorption, and provided enhanced cellular uptake and cytotoxicity in cultured cancer cells. Subsequent studies showed that this nanoparticle delivery strategy circumvents resistance that arises from lowered expression levels of the copper transporter CTR1 and decreases the interaction of the platinum complexes with biological deactivation agents, such as metallothionein and glutathione.461
## 8.3. Other Inorganic Nanoparticles
Conjugation of a platinum(IV) prodrug to a nanoparticle can provide benefits that extend beyond those simply related to delivery. The photoactivatable trans platinum(IV) complexes described above are often limited by the need to absorb high energy light to be activated. Advancements have been made through judicious ligand choice, but in an alternative strategy, trans,trans,trans- $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{P t}(\mathbf{N}_{3})_{2}(\mathbf{N}\mathrm{H}_{3})(\mathsf{p y},$ ) $\mathrm{\langleO_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H\rangle_{2}]}$ (Chart 20A) was conjugated to a core−shell upconversion nanoparticle.462 These nanoparticles, composed of a core of $\mathrm{NaYF}_{4}$ doped with ytterbium(III) and thulium(III) surrounded by a shell of ${\mathrm{NaGdF}}_{4}$ doped with ytterbium(III), absorb 980 nm laser light and emit at wavelengths of 291, 346, and $363\mathrm{nm}$ . Irradiation of buffered suspensions of the platinum-bearing, PEGylated nanoparticle construct for $30~\mathrm{\min}$ liberated approximately one-half of the conjugated platinum. In vitro cytotoxicity studies confirmed that the platinum released by $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ irradiation is toxic toward cancer cells. In a mouse xenograft model of murine hepatocarcinoma, tumor-bearing mice received an intratumoral injection of the nanoparticle construct. The tumor site was then irradiated with no light, UV light, or $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ light. UV light can activate the platinum complex but has weaker tissue penetration than near-IR light, which is able to activate the platinum(IV) complex by upconversion luminescence. Consequently, the tumors in the mice irradiated with UV light grew to a greater extent than those irradiated with $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ light. The average tumor size in the latter group did not increase, even over the course of 2 weeks. In a related system, silica-coated upconversion nanoparticles of $\mathrm{NaYF}_{4}$ doped with ytterbium(III) and thulium(III) were conjugated to trans,trans,trans- $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{P t}(\mathbf N_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})$ - $(\mathrm{O_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}\mathrm{\bar{C}O_{2}H)(p y)_{2}]}}$ (Chart 20B), through a bridging peptide, as well as a fluorescent apoptosis-sensing peptide.463 Near-IR irradiation of this construct could activate the platinum(IV) prodrug and induce apoptosis in cultured cancer cells that were both cisplatin-sensitive and cisplatin-resistant.
Platinum(IV) prodrugs with cis or trans azide or iodide ligands have been designed as photoreactive pro-drugs, a strategy used in constructs described above. It is widely known, however, that many platinum(IV) complexes without azide or iodide ligands are sensitive to photodecomposition. To exploit this reactivity as a route toward photoactivation of platinum(IV) prodrugs in general, quantum dots were investigated as photosensitizers. Quantum dots are semiconductor nanoparticles having exceptional electronic properties that vary with the size of the nanostructure. They have been extensively explored as photosensitizers for photodynamic therapy.464 In a proof-of-concept study, $\big[\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}(\mathrm{bpy})\big]$ , where byp is $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ - bipyridine, was suspended in organic solvents with $\mathrm{CdSe}/\mathrm{ZnS}$ core−shell quantum dots and irradiated with 530 nm light.465 $\big[\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}\big(\mathrm{bpy}\big)\big]$ was released, as monitored by electronic absorption spectroscopy. $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectroscopic measurements indicate that the hydrophobic platinum complex interacts with the hydrophobic surface of the quantum dots, and the authors suggest that this interaction facilitates a photoinduced electron transfer from the quantum dot to the platinum complex.
To render the system more biologically relevant, micelles packed with $\mathrm{CdSe}/\mathrm{ZnS}$ quantum dots were prepared by addition of phospholipids and $\mathrm{PEG}_{2000}$ .466 The photosensitized reduction of a more relevant prodrug, cis,cis,trans-[Pt$\left(\mathrm{NH_{3}}\right)_{2}\mathrm{Cl_{2}}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CCH_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H})_{2}]$ (Chart 20C), was investigated. As in the earlier study, NMR spectroscopic measurements revealed an interaction between the prodrug and the quantum dots. When a colloidal suspension of the selfassembled micellar structures in an aqueous solution of the prodrug was irradiated with either 480 or $630\ \mathrm{nm}$ light, the platinum(IV) complex was reduced with loss of the two axial ligands. Control studies confirmed that the presence of the quantum dots was required for efficient photoreduction, and Xray photoelectron spectroscopic (XPS) studies of the platinum $4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ and $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ peaks confirmed that, after irradiation, the platinum was present in the $^{2+}$ oxidation state. In vitro cytotoxicity assays showed that neither the prodrug $\langle\mathrm{IC}_{50}\approx$ $500~\mu\mathrm{M})$ nor the quantum dot-filled micelles alone displayed significant toxicity, but that nanomolar concentrations of the dots combined with irradiation led to an $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ of $25\ \mu\mathrm{M}.$ Extensive theoretical calculations were carried out to probe the mechanism of photoactivation of cisplatin prodrugs by quantum dots. The results are consistent with a model in which the platinum(IV) complex interacts with the nanoparticle surface, and computation of the electronic coupling between the donor and acceptor indicates that the electrons injected into the platinum(IV) complex produce an excited state that leads specifically to dissociation of the succinate ligands.467
Although we have not found any reports of platinum(IV) prodrugs conjugated to quantum dot drug-delivery vehicles, we anticipate that such a development is forthcoming. Lanthanide upconversion nanoparticle constructs in which cis,cis,trans$\mathrm{\small\left[\bar{P}t(N H_{3})_{2}C l_{2}(O_{2}C\bar{C}H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H)_{2}\right]}$ (Chart 20C) is conjugated to a phospholipid-functionalized PEG chain have, however, been prepared. Specifically, the platinated polymer was used to generate stable aqueous suspensions of thulium(III)-doped $\mathrm{NaYF_{4}}$ :Yb(III) upconversion nanoparticles.468 Irradiation of this construct with $980~\mathrm{nm}$ light not only led to release of succinate, as expected because of the photolabilization of the axial ligands, but also reduced all of the platinum centers to the platinum(II) oxidation state as determined by XPS.
Upconversion nanoparticles have also been used as a nanodelivery vehicle in which the lanthanide-based luminescence is not implicated in the release or activity of the platinum agent. A cisplatin-releasing platinum(IV) prodrug was conjugated to the surface of a poly(ethylenimine)-coated $\mathrm{NaYF}_{4}$ nanoparticle doped with ytterbium(III) and erbium(III) through a succinate axial ligand.469 The nanoparticle was further functionalized with a folic acid targeting group. Cellular uptake of the nanoparticles was monitored using the inherent luminescent properties of the nanoparticles, and cisplatin was released upon intracellular reduction.
Hydrophobic iron oxide nanoparticles can be encapsulated in gelatin to enhance their water solubility. The amine functionalities on gelatin allowed the platinum(IV) prodrug cis,cis,trans- $\left[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{-})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CCH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\bar{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}\mathrm{H})_{2}\right]$ (Chart 20C) to be conjugated to the surface along with a fluorescent marker, fluorescein isothiocyanate.470 Release of platinum was not explicitly measured but rather was inferred from the photometrically quantitated release of the fluorophore. The release could be enhanced by the presence of an undefined pancreatic enzyme, which we assume to be trypsin. The superparamagnetic properties of the nanoparticles were used to generate $\mathbf{T}_{2}$ -weighted magnetic resonance images showing contrast in the tumor region following intratumoral injection.
Layered double hydroxides are inorganic materials in which positively charged layers are interspersed with loosely associated charge-balancing anions.471 These anions in the interlayer space can often be readily exchanged. Indeed, cis,cis,trans- $\mathrm{\small\left[\bar{P}t(N H_{3})_{2}C l_{2}(O_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H)_{2}\right]}$ (Chart 20C) was loaded into layered double hydroxide nanoparticles of the formula $\big[\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}_{0.66}\mathrm{Al^{3+}}_{0.34}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\big]\mathrm{Cl^{-}}_{0.34}{\cdot}2.7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ simply by incubating the material in a $\mathrm{pH}8$ aqueous solution of the prodrug for 1 day. Platinum loading did not change the morphology of the particles, although it did cause an approximate doubling in the average diameter of the particles as determined by dynamic light scattering. Platinum incorporation was measured using atomic absorption spectroscopy, and a slight decrease in the zeta potential of the material was taken as corroborating evidence of the inclusion of the negatively charged succinatebearing prodrug into the material. The nanoparticle construct was more effective at killing cancer cells than cisplatin alone, and the former had reduced toxicity in noncancerous immortalized cell lines. Mechanistic studies confirmed that the construct acts by a mechanism analogous to that of cisplatin.
## 8.4. Coordination Polymers
In an alternative strategy, the disuccinate complex described above in the preparation of the folate-targeted SWCNT (Chart 18) was used to create coordination polymers that precipitated from solution as nanoparticles.472 A $\bar{\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}$ ion was employed to cross-link the carboxylate functional groups of different platinum complexes (Chart 21A). ICP-MS and thermal gravimetric analysis measurements confirmed the empirical formula of the coordination polymer to be $\mathrm{Tb}_{2}\big(\mathrm{Pt}^{\mathrm{IV}}\big)_{3}\big(\hat{\mathrm{H}_{2}}\mathrm{O}\big)_{12}$ w h e r e P t I V r e p r e s e n t s c i s , c i s , t r a n s - [ P t ( N H 3 ) 2 C l 2 − $\left(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CCH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H}\right)_{2}.$ . The stability of the nanoparticles in suspension could be significantly enhanced with a silica shell coating. Moreover, a silyl-derived c(RGDfK) cyclic peptide could be grafted to the silica surface of the coated nanoparticles, which targeted the construct to cells that express the $\alpha_{\mathrm{V}}\beta_{3}$ integrin, such as HT-29, preferentially over those that do not, such as MCF-7.
Chart 21. Depiction of the Formation of Coordination Polymers Using Metal Units to Link Platinum(IV) Prodrugs Bearing Axial Ligands with Coordinating Motifs
A variation on this theme appeared in the report of nanoscale coordination polymers formed from platinum(IV) prodrugs bearing pendent phosphonates and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2\bar{+}}$ ions.473 The platinum(IV) prodrugs featured either a cisplatin or an oxaliplatin equatorial core and axial phosphonylcarbamate ligands. The phosphonate moiety permits self-assembly with $Z\mathrm{\overline{{n}}}^{2+}$ to form extended coordination polymer networks, which precipitate from solution in a nanoparticulate form (Chart 21B). The particles were stabilized and rendered biocompatible by PEGylation using a phospholipid, cholesterol, and a PEGylated phospholipid. Fluorescently labeled analogs of these particles were observed to enter the cell by fluorescence microscopy. In vitro experiments with cultured cancer cells confirmed the ability of the construct to induce the DNA damage characteristic of the parent platinum drugs and trigger apoptosis. Further in vitro studies with inhibitors of endocytosis and fluorescent dyes that selectively localize to endosomal and lysosomal compartments confirmed that uptake occurred through energydependent endocytotic processes. Pharmacokinetics studies in mice revealed that the nanoparticle formulation provided blood circulation times that were more than 40-fold greater than those of the parent drugs. In mouse xenograft models of nonsmall cell lung cancer and pancreatic cancer, these constructs were able to inhibit tumor growth better than the parent platinum(II) drugs. In a very recent development with this delivery platform, the cisplatin-delivering nanoconstruct was prepared using an alternative lipid to afford nanoparticles with an overall positive charge concentrated near the surface of the particle but below the surface of the outer PEG layer.474 Electrostatic interactions were used to load negatively charged siRNAs into the particle. Three distinct genes were targeted by the siRNAs: survivin, bcl-2, and $p$ -gp. The construct released active platinum agents and the siRNAs in a controlled fashion, all of which were able to carry out their intended biological functions in vitro. In a mouse xenograft model of ovarian cancer, the codelivery enhanced the anticancer activity of the platinum agent as evidenced by inhibition of tumor growth, reduced expression of the silenced proteins in tumor tissue, and increased evidence of apoptosis in tumor cells.
## 8.5. Metal−Organic Frameworks
As an extension of the Pt−Tb coordination polymer work that was described above, platinum conjugates of nanosized metal− organic frameworks (MOFs) were prepared.475 In these instances, the platinum complex does not act as a structural component of the coordination polymer, which is instead formed from a first-row transition metal and an aminofunctionalized terephthalate. The nonplatinum metal center combines with the aromatic dicarboxylate to form the extended three-dimensional MOF structure. Iron was used to form a nanoparticulate MOF, and cis,cis,trans- $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{OEt}).$ - $\mathrm{\Gamma(O_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H)}\mathrm{\Gamma}$ ] was attached to the pendent amine following activation with $^{1,1}$ -carbonyldiimidazole.476 As with the $\mathrm{{Pt-Tb}}$ coordination polymer, aqueous stability of the nanoparticles was enhanced with a coating of amorphous silica, although alternative chemistry using ${\bf N a}_{2}{\bf S i O}_{3}$ as the silica source was required to avoid decomposition of the particles. The silica shell also provided a more controlled release of platinum from the particle. No significant enhancement in activity was obtained, however, in the in vitro cytotoxicity tests. In a subsequent iteration of this drug-delivery strategy, a zirconium(IV) containing MOF was prepared using aminotriphenyldicarboxylate.477 The similarity of the organic bridging ligands to the amino-functionalized terephthalate in the system above would suggest that the nanoscale MOF could be p o s t s y n t h e t i c a l l y m o d i fi e d w i t h c i s , c i s , t r a n s - [ P t - $(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ (OEt) $\mathrm{\tilde{O}_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H)}\mathrm{\tilde{\Lambda}}$ ] using amide bond forming reactions, but the authors instead simply incubate the platinum complex with the MOF to load the nanostructure through noncovalent interactions. This mode of encapsulation was confirmed using $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectroscopy. The survivin/Bcl$2/\mathrm{P-gp}$ siRNA cocktail described above (section 8.4) was also loaded into the nanoscale MOF, and the encapsulation was proposed to proceed through coordination of the sugar− phosphate backbone to the zirconium centers. Protection of the encapsulated siRNAs from degradation by nucleases was observed, and the combined delivery of the platinum agent and the siRNAs provided a chemotherapeutic enhancement of over 10-fold in the in vitro cytotoxicity assays.
## 8.6. Polysiloxane
Incorporation of a platinum(IV) prodrug as an integral component of a polymeric polysilsesquioxane nanoparticle led to improved stability of the particle.478 These nanoparticles form from the anionic reverse microemulsion base-catalyzed sol-polymerization of platinum(IV) prodrugs bearing axial ligands with pendent trialkoxysilanes. The nanoparticles function as effective controlled-release agents of oxaliplatin, evolving $80\%$ of the encapsulated payload over the course of 2 days upon incubation with cysteine. PEGylation was used to enhance the biocompatibility of the nanoparticles, which were further functionalized with anisamide to target the sigma receptor. This opioid receptor is overexpressed on the surfaces of many types of cancers cells.479 An integrin-targeting RGD unit could also be attached to the nanoparticle surface, enhancing efficacy both in vitro and in vivo. A cisplatindelivering analog of this construct (Chart 22) was also developed and tested for its ability to improve upon cisplatinmediated tumor growth inhibition when administered in conjunction with radiation therap y.480 In vitro and in vivo studies, the latter in a xenograft model of nonsmall cell lung cancer, suggest that this cisplatin-delivering polysilsesquioxane nanoparticle can offer significant improvements over conventional chemoradiation therapy using cisplatin.
## 8.7. Polymeric Micelles
A highly successful platform that has been exploited in the nanodelivery of platinum(IV) prodrugs comprises polymeric micellar nanoparticles that form from the self-assembly of amphiphilic block copolymers. When an organic solution of such polymers, which contain a hydrophobic block and a hydrophilic block, is added to water, the hydrophobic portions of the chains cluster to form a hydrophobic core, which is surrounded by a shell formed from the hydrophilic portions of the copolymer chains. If this self-assembly process occurs in the presence of a hydrophobic drug-like molecule, then it can be encapsulated within the core of the nanoparticle, which then serves as a controlled-release drug delivery device. As opposed to the constructs described above, in which the axial ligands of the platinum(IV) prodrug were chosen to permit covalent conjugation, the axial ligands of the prodrug can be used to tune hydrophobicity, a key parameter in nanoencapsulation. Another strategy, similar to that used in the delivery devices described in previous subsections, involves covalent conjugation of the platinum(IV) complex to the polymer backbone and subsequent nanoparticle formation. Examples of these two strategies will now be treated sequentially.
8.7.1. Polymer Micelles: Noncovalent Encapsulation of Platinum(IV) Prodrugs. One copolymer system that has been extensively investigated is poly(lactic-co-glycolic acid)- block-poly(ethylene glycol) or PLGA−PEG (Chart 23), in which the statistical copolymer PLGA serves as the hydrophobic block and PEG as the hydrophilic block. PLGA is a biocompatible, biodegradable polymer that is approved for use by the FDA for a variety of biomedical devices.481 The safety of PEG has long been investigated, and the FDA has declared that it is “generally recognized as safe”.482,483 PLGA nanoparticles have been widely explored for drug delivery applications.481 The current popularity of the PLGA−PEG block copolymer has led a number of commercial vendors to offer PLGA−PEG with a variety of block sizes and variations in the relative ratio of lactic acid to glycolic acid in the PLGA block. These parameters influence the properties of the nanoparticles formed from the polymer. Recent work has even investigated the influence of the ordering of the lactic acid and glycolic acid units within the PLGA block.484,485
T h e p l a t i n u m ( I V ) p r o d r u g c i s , c i s , t r a n s - [ P t - $(\mathrm{NH_{3}})_{2}\mathrm{C\bar{l}_{2}(O_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C H_{2}\bar{C}H_{2}C H_{3})_{2}}]$ (Chart 23C), which bears an equatorial cisplatin core and axial hexanoate ligands, was encapsulated within the hydrophobic core of a nanoparticle formed from PLGA−PEG−COOH. This polymer is a derivative of PLGA−PEG in which the exposed end of the PEG chain is functionalized with a carboxylic acid. This pendent carboxylic acid was then used to conjugate a targeting unit to the surface of the platinum-loaded nanoparticle. An RNA aptamer that could recognize the prostate-specific membrane antigen (PSMA) was used and provided selective targeting of the construct to LNCaP prostate cancer cells that abundantly express this membrane protein. PSMA is highly expressed in many prostate tumors, particularly in the metastatic and hormone-refractory forms.486 Fluorescence microscopy confirmed that the construct was taken up by endocytosis, and immunofluorescence imaging using the R-C18 antibody confirmed formation of $1,2\mathrm{-d}(\mathrm{Gp}\mathrm{\bar{G}})$ intrastrand DNA cross-links.
The construct exhibited submicromolar $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ values in cultured human prostate cancer cells. Subsequent studies with Swiss albino mice and Sprague−Dawley rats demonstrated that this prodrug-loaded nanoparticle construct has enhanced pharmacokinetics, biodistribution, and tolerability as compared to that of cisplatin.487 In a murine model of prostate cancer in which BALB/c nude mice were injected with cultured LNCaP cells to form a subcutaneous xenograft, the nanoparticle construct was able to provide an equivalent degree of reduction in tumor size as a 3-fold higher molar dose of cisplatin. The enhanced activity was attributed to a combination of passive targeting of tumor tissue arising from the EPR effect, active targeting of the PSMA-expressing LNCaP cells by the aptamer conjugated to the surface, and prolonged residence of the platinum species in the blood. This nanoparticle platform was also used to prepare platinum-loaded constructs that were functionalized with the cyclic pentapeptide c(RGDfK).488 This unit allowed the nanoparticles to target angiogenic blood vessels in an orthotopic breast cancer xenograft model. In this model, the nanoparticle was more efficacious and better tolerated than cisplatin.
A series of complexes of the form cis,cis,trans-[Pt$\mathrm{(NH_{3})_{2}C l_{2}(O_{2}C(C H_{2})_{{n}}C H_{3})_{2}]}$ (Chart 23C), of which the hexanoate complex described above is a member, were prepared to systematically investigate the effect of the length of the polymethylene chain of the alkyl carboxylate axial ligands on nanoencapsulation.489 This study revealed that increasing the length of the chain increases platinum loading into the nanoparticle but also increases the propensity for aggregation and macroscopic precipitation. An optimal balance was struck using a $4{:}6~\mathrm{w}/\mathrm{w}$ mixture of the PLGA−PEG polymer and the decanoate complex cis,cis,trans- $\mathsf{\Gamma}\big[\mathrm{Pt}\big(\mathrm{N\bar{H}}_{3}\big)_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\big(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{C}\cdot$ $\left(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\right)_{8}\mathrm{CH}_{3}\right)_{2}]$ . This complex was then used as the prodrug component of a nanoparticle platform designed to deliver cisplatin and siRNAs capable of suppressing the function of REV1 and ${\mathrm{REV}}3{\mathrm{L}},$ which are involved in the process of errorprone translesion DNA synthesis.490 Such translesion synthesis can contribute to cisplatin resistance in tumors.491 The nanoparticle construct was formed from the interaction of PLGA−PEG, a cationic lipid, the platinum prodrug, and the siRNA. The PLGA block interacted with the cationic lipid to form a polymer matrix in which the prodrug was suspended and the PEG block provided an outer shell. The nanoparticles were formulated using a double emulsion strategy (vide infra), which allowed the PLGA−lipid matrix to surround an aqueous core in which the siRNA molecules were dissolved. Sustained release of the platinum and siRNA was achieved, and the released siRNAs were able to decrease expression of their target genes both in vitro and in vivo. In a LNCaP xenograft model, inclusion of the siRNA rendered the tumors more susceptible to platinumbased therapy.
A platform for preparing platinum(IV) prodrugs that can be readily conjugated to another chemical moiety using strainpromoted azide−alkyne cycloaddition, one of the so-called “copper-free click chemistries”, was recently developed.492 Although $\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{IV})$ complexes can be unstable in the presence of reductants like the ascorbate ions typically employed in Cucatalyzed click reactions, examples of successful functionalization using $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ salts in organic solvents have been reported.493,494 As a proof of principle, a platinum(IV) prodrug with axial ligands displaying pendent azide units was coupled to a functionalized azadibenzocyclooctyne (ADIBO) (Chart 23D). The significant increase in lipophilicity upon reaction with the strained cyclooctyne prompted the authors to investigate the nanoencapsulation of this complex in PLGA− PEG-based nanoparticles. The ADIBO-functionalized cisplatin prodrug encapsulated far better than the unmodified, azideterminated platinum complex. This platform also holds significant promise for conjugating platinum prodrugs to a variety of other nanodelivery devices, targeting units, bioactive molecules, and reporter beacons. In one such application, coupling of the platinum(IV) azide-bearing complex to a cyclooctyne-modified triphenylphosphonium salt afforded a platinum(IV) prodrug that targets mitochondria.495 This complex was then encapsulated within a PLGA−PEG nanoparticle, which itself was functionalized with a triphenylphosphonium unit. In vitro studies confirmed the ability of this construct to accumulate in the mitochondria of cultured cancer cells, disrupt their altered mitochondrial metabolism, and induce cell death. The ability of the triphenylphosphoniumderivatized nanoparticles to penetrate the blood−brain barrier led to investigations into the potency of this construct against neuroblastoma cells. Encouragingly, this work showed that the nanoparticle construct was approximately 17-fold more active than cisplatin.
Instead of using synthetic high polymers, nanoparticles formed from polymers of natural origin have also been explored for the delivery of platinum(IV) prodrugs. Silk fibroin (Chart 23) is the fibrous protein component of the silk made by spiders, silkworks, and other insects. This biocompatible, biodegradable material has been successfully employed in a range of biomedical applications from sutures to threedimensional tissue scaffolding,496,497 and silk fibroin can be formed into nanoparticles for drug delivery.498 To improve upon an initial nanoparticle design in which cisplatin was loaded into silk fibroin nanoparticles by coordination of the platinum(II) complex to the polymer,499 a new construct was very recently reported in which the hydrophobic prodrug cis,cis,trans- $\mathrm{\small{[\bar{P}t(N\bar{H}_{3})_{2}C l_{2}(O_{2}C C_{6}H_{5})_{2}]}}$ (Chart 23G) was encapsulated within such nanoparticles.500 Unlike many of the other polymer-based delivery systems described here, the platinum loading is not accomplished simultaneously with nanoparticle formation, but rather a dried sample of preformed nanoparticles is suspended in a DMSO solution of the platinum complex to load the prodrug into the nanoparticles. TEM images support the cellular internalization of the nanoparticle constructs, and in vitro assays indicate that the construct is effective at killing cultured cancer cells. Flow cytometric methods were used to carry out cell cycle analyses that confirm a mechanism of action similar to that of cisplatin is operative. Although increasing the lipophilicity of a platinum(IV) prodrug by increasing the hydrophobic character of the axial ligands is an effective way to influence nanoencapsulation, this strategy might not be applicable to dual-threat complexes in which the axial ligands, selected to elicit a particular biological response, render the complex hydrophilic. For instance, mitaplatin (Chart 23A) is relatively water-soluble and does not readily encapsulate within PLGA−PEG using conventional nanoprecipitation techniques. Moreover, changing any of the ligands to increase lipophilicity would compromise the activity of the platinum(II) or DCA species released. Instead, an alternative encapsulation strategy, which had previously been employed to encapsulate hydrophilic species like proteins, was investigated.501 Using a water-in-oil-in-water double-emulsion solvent evaporation strategy, mitaplatin could be encapsulated within nanoparticles formed from PLGA−PEG.389 This nanoencapsulation formulation afforded mitaplatin an increased residence time in the bloodstream and decreased accumulation in the kidneys without negatively impacting anticancer activity in a mouse xenograft model of triple-negative breast cancer. A dual-threat complex might fortuitously have properties such that the axial ligands permit facile incorporation within the hydrophobic core of a polymeric micelle. Such is the case for canthaplatin (Chart 23B),502 a cisplatin prodrug in which the axial ligands are derivatives of the protein phosphatase 2A inhibitor demethylcantharidin, the hydrolyzed form of which is endothall. This work builds upon the earlier report of endothall-containing platinum(IV) prodrugs described above. The Boc-protected pipirazinyl groups on the axial canthaplatinderived ligands allow the prodrug to be readily encapsulated in PLGA−PEG nanoparticles, affording a construct that is taken up by endocytosis, decreases the efficiency of DNA repair by inhibiting protein phosphatase 2A, and releases cisplatin. Enhanced efficacy resulting from inhibited DNA repair was confirmed in vitro and in vivo with a mouse xenograft model of lung cancer.
In another instance, a cisplatin prodrug was prepared in which a paclitaxel derivative was installed at one axial position through a platinum-coordinated glutaric acid (Chart 23F), but no significant enhancement over cotreatment with the unencapsulated species was observed.503 The platinum(IV) prodrug VAAP (section 8) was encapsulated within a poly(caprolactone)-PEG polymeric nanoparticle (Chart 23E).373 The hydrophobic valproate axial ligands not only permit encapsulation, but upon reductive release are capable of acting as histone deacetylase inhibitors. This enzyme inhibitory activity, which can potentiate the activity of cisplatin,504 was observed in the in vitro experiments using VAAP alone, as described above. In mice, the nanoparticle formulation significantly enhanced retention of platinum in the bloodstream. Murine xenograft studies with a human lung cancer model did not reveal any significant enhancement in tumor reduction in the nanoparticle-treated batch as compared to tumor-bearing animals treated with unencapsulated VAAP. Encapsulation did, however, enhance platinum accumulation in the tumor and reduce kidney toxicity, as assessed by histological analysis of renal tissue.
8.7.2. Polymer Micelles: Covalent Conjugation of Platinum(IV) Prodrugs. Instead of relying on noncovalent interactions to associate a platinum complex with a polymer nanoparticle, the axial ligands of a platinum(IV) prodrug can be covalently linked to a polymer chain using well-established coupling chemistry. The first example of covalent conjugation of a platinum(IV) complex to a polymer chain for subsequent formation of a polymeric micelle exploited the ability of a cisplatin prodrug with axial levulinate ligands to react with the end group of a hydrazine-terminated poly(ethylene glycol)- block-poly(L-lactic acid) (PLA−PEG).505 The hydrazine group caps the hydrophobic block, and in the resulting platinated polymer the complex is linked to the macromolecule through a hydrazone. The polymer chains can self-assemble into micelles with the platinum buried in the hydrophobic core. The rate of platinum release from the nanoparticle construct varies with pH because of the acid-lability of the hydrazone linkage. Efficacy was demonstrated in vitro using cultured cancer cells.
In a very recent variation on this model, the termini of the hydrophobic blocks of a PLA−PEG copolymer were crosslinked by esterification with the succinate ligands of cis,cis,trans$\left[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{NH_{3}})_{2}\mathrm{Cl_{2}}(\mathrm{O_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H})_{2}\right]$ .506 These conjugates formed micelles in aqueous solution that undergo a thermoreversible sol−gel transition, forming hydrogels at 37 ${}^{\circ}{\bf C}.$ . These semisolids were capable of releasing platinum in a controlled fashion, without an initial burst, over the course of 2 months. The platinum is mainly released in a micellar form, which is taken up by endocytosis and demonstrates enhanced toxicity in cultured cancer cells as compared to cisplatin.
The use of platinum(IV) complexes with succinate axial ligands to conjugate prodrugs to the polymer backbone using ester- or amide-forming reactions is a recurring theme within this class of constructs. In the first report of this strategy, cis,cis,trans- $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{OH}$ ) $\mathrm{\bar{O}_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H)}$ ] was conjugated to a PLA derivative bearing pendent hydroxyl functional groups.507 Using hydrodynamic flow focusing,508 nanoparticles comprising a blend of this platinated polymer and PLGA−PEG−COOH were formed. If the nanoparticle formation was carried out in the presence of docetaxel, then this molecule was encapsulated within the hydrophobic core of the nanoparticles, affording a construct that can deliver two chemotherapeutics by orthogonal release mechanisms. The docetaxel is released by passive diffusion from the nanoparticle, whereas the stable covalent bond of the platinum complex to the polymer chain permits release of the platinum only upon reduction of the metal center. Surface modification of the assembled nanoparticle with a PSMA-targeting aptamer allowed for enhanced uptake by cultured prostate cancer cells. A similar construct was developed in which the drug delivered along with the platinum was irinotecan.509 A particularly low polydispersity among the nanoparticles was again achieved by carrying out nanoprecipitation using microfluidic devices. Targeting with the PSMA aptamer again allowed for enhanced uptake by cells expressing this antigen. Inclusion of irinotecan in the particles provided an enhancement over nanoparticles containing only the cisplatin prodrug.
In the study described above in which PLGA−PEG particles were designed to release both cisplatin and docetaxel, the cisplatin prodrug was covalently conjugated to the polymer backbone, and the docetaxel was noncovalently encapsulated within the nanoparticle core. In an alternative strategy, two separate polymer chains were covalently modified with different anticancer agents, an oxaliplatin prodrug bearing a pendent carboxylate and daunomycin.510 The former can be released by reduction and the latter by hydrolysis. The novel amphiphilic block copolymer used for this study was poly(lactide-co-2- methyl-2-carboxyl-propylene carbonate)-block-poly(ethylene glycol). Composite nanoparticles were formed from combinations of the two polymer chains, and the ratio of the two anticancer agents was tuned by changing the relative amounts of the two modified chains during nanoparticle formation. Facile variation of this ratio allowed the authors to readily interrogate synergistic effects in vitro and in vivo. A similar construct was investigated in which the platinum agent was a cisplatin prodrug and the second anticancer agent was a paclitaxel conjugate.511 This polymer platform was also used to prepare a conjugate with cis,cis,trans- $[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{NH_{3}})_{2}\mathrm{Cl_{2}}(\mathrm{OH}).$ - $\left(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CCHCl}_{2}\right)]$ , a platinum(IV) prodrug designed to release cisplatin and DCA, much like mitaplatin. This strategy provides an alternative to the double emulsion strategy and permits nanodelivery of a hydrophilic complex.512
Using the same poly(lactide-co-2-methyl-2-carboxyl-propylene carbonate)-block-poly(ethylene glycol), a nanoparticle construct was prepared in which the platinum(IV) prodrug conjugated to the polymer backbone displays the photoactivatable cis-diazide motif.513 Fluorescence microscopy studies were used to confirm that the nanoparticles were taken up by endocytosis, and the authors emphasize that this route of cellular uptake provides a means of circumventing resistance related to expression levels of copper transporters, proteins for which the role in the activity of and resistance to cisplatin has been extensively studied.21 The particles, which are stable in the dark, exhibit fast release of platinum upon irradiation with UV light. A variety of pharmacokinetic parameters were evaluated in Chinese KM mice, which indicated that treatment with the nanoparticle construct followed by UV irradiation resulted in much lower systemic toxicity than treatment with cisplatin.
In addition to the diblock copolymers described above, a triblock amphiphilic copolymer was also developed to deliver platinum(IV) prodrugs. This polymer is nontoxic and biodegradable, comprising a methoxy-terminated poly(ethylene glycol) block, a poly( $\varepsilon$ -caprolactone) block, and a poly(Llysine) block.514,515 This polymer was platinated with cis,cis,trans- $\mathrm{\small{[Pt(NH_{3})_{2}C l_{2}(O H)(O_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H)]}}$ through amide bond forming reactions with the lysine side chains and self-assembled into micellar nanoparticles. These nanoparticles released platinum(IV) complexes upon exposure to acidic conditions and liberated platinum(II) complexes upon reduction. In vitro assays confirmed that the nanoparticles are taken up by endocytosis, affording enhanced intracellular platinum accumulation and cytotoxicity. A series of photoactivatable platinum(IV) complexes with cis azide ligands trans to either cis amines or a chelating 1R,2R-DACH were also conjugated to the polymer.516 These constructs were stable in the dark, but upon irradiation with UV light released cytotoxic platinum(II) species. Studies with cultured cancer cells verified the light-induced cytotoxic activity of the constructs. The nanoparticle functionalized with cis,trans-[Pt(DACH)- $\mathrm{\Gamma(N_{3})_{2}(O H)(O_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H)_{\it\cdot}}$ ], the most effective construct in the series, was carried forward for testing in a xenograft model of murine hepatocarcinoma. Enhanced tumor growth inhibition was observed when mice were injected intratumorally with the construct and the tumor was irradiated with UV light for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . An additional hour of UV irradiation was administered 5 days postinjection. The combination of nanoparticle treatment and UV irradiation caused less of an effect on body weight than treatment with an equivalent dose of oxaliplatin.
Using this same polymer system, a nanoparticle construct was developed that is capable of releasing carboplatin upon reduction of the platinum(IV) center.517 The carboplatin prodrug was attached to the polymer backbone through an axial succinate ligand. In the same report, an alternative method of nanoparticle-mediated codelivery of cisplatin and DCA was described. As opposed to mitaplatin, which bears axial DCA ligands, a platinum(IV) complex was prepared by hydrogen peroxide oxidation of cis- $[\bar{\mathrm{Pt}}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}\bar{(\mathrm{DCA})_{2}]}$ . Subsequent derivatization afforded an axial succinate, through which the complex could be tethered to the polymer backbone using amide bond forming reactions. An enhancement in activity was noted by comparison to the carboplatin-delivering polymer or cotreatment with unencapsulated carboplatin and DCA. This result is not surprising given the greater kinetic inertness of the chelating cyclobutanedicarboxylate ligand of carboplatin as compared to the monodentate carboxylate DCA. Dinuclear platinum complexes, analogous to those described in section 4,518 were also delivered by covalent conjugation of the corresponding platinum(IV) prodrugs to the polymer backbone.519 Compounds with both ammine and 1R,2R-DACH nonleaving group ligands were prepared bearing axial carboxylates. Coupling to the polymer was achieved though amide bond forming reactions with the lysine amines.
Very recently, another construct based on this polymer was reported.520 Camplatin, a platinum(IV) prodrug derived from oxidized cisplatin and camphoric anhydride, was conjugated to the pendent amine groups of the poly(L-lysine) block. Quantification of the mRNA levels of Bcl-2 and Bax revealed that treatment with the nanoparticle-encapsulated camplatin decreased production of these proteins in cultured ovarian cancer cells.
Micellar nanoparticles formed from amphiphilic block copolymers of the types described above appear to be stable enough to maintain their structural integrity and elicit characteristic biological responses, particularly in vivo. The classical theory of micelles states, however, that in solution an equilibrium occurs between the self-assembled structure and deassembled unimers. Chemical cross-linking of the polymer chains within the core or the shell can be carried out to improve micellar stability.521 Because overly stable micelles can prevent efficient release of encapsulated active agents, stimuliresponsive cross-linked micelles have been developed that are able to release a cytotoxic payload upon, for instance, entry into the acidic microenvironment of the tumor.522 A platinumdelivering polymeric micelle based on a poly[(2-(2- methoxyethoxy)ethyl methacrylate)- $.c o$ -( $\mathrm{\Delta}N.$ -methacryloxy-3-azido p r o py la mi d e)] -bloc k- p o l y ( $N$ -(2-hydroxypropyl)- methacrylamide) block copolymer was prepared.523 The pendent azide groups were functionalized with both the platinum(IV) prodrug cis,cis,trans- $\mathrm{\Gamma[Pt(NH_{3})_{2}C l_{2}(O H)-}$ $\left(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CCH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CONHCH}_{2}\mathrm{CCH}\right)_{\cdot}$ ] and the near-IR dye cypate, for combination platinum/photothermal therapy. The attachment of the warheads to the polymer backbone was accomplished using Cu-catalyzed alkyne−azide cycloaddition. The authors of the study described above, in which strainpromoted alkyne−azide cycloaddition was used to functionalize platinum(IV) complexes, noted that their use of the strainpromotion strategy was driven by the propensity for Cucatalyzed click reactions to reduce platinum(IV) centers. In this instance, the successful use of the latter coupling strategy might stem from the use of pentamethyldiethylenetriamine as a Cuchelating agent. We also note briefly that strategies have been developed to extend the use of “click” technologies to $\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{II})$ species, using Cu-catalyzed or Cu-free azide−alkyne cycloaddition and Pt-mediated amine-to-nitrile addition.524−528 Chemical cross-linking of the cores of the micelles formed from these functionalized polymers was carried out with a cystamine cross-linker, producing a construct that is sensitive to reducing environments, such as the cytoplasm of cancer cells. In vitro studies confirmed the ability of the construct to release its cargo in a controlled fashion and elicit chemotherapy/ photothermal therapy synergy.
The platinum complex itself can act as the chemical crosslinking agent. Following functionalization of the axial succinate ligands of cis,cis,trans- $\mathrm{\cdot\big[Pt\big(NH_{3}\big)_{2}C l_{2}\big(O_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H\big)_{2}\big]}$ with ethylenediamine, the platinum complex was used to cross-link the cores of polymeric micelles formed from poly(oligo(ethylene glycol)methyl ether methacrylate)-blockpoly(styrene-co-3-isopropenyl- $_{\alpha,\alpha}$ -dimethylbenzyl isocyanate).529 The facile reaction between the isocyante groups on the polymer and the pendent amine groups of the platinum complex afforded controlled-release nanoparticles. The same complex was used without ethylenediamine functionalization to cross-link the core of the biodegradable polymer monomethoxy poly(ethylene glycol)-block-poly(L-lysine) by reaction of the lysine amine groups with the carboxylic acids of the prodrug.530 Analogously, core-cross-linked micelles formed from the triblock copolymer monomethoxyl poly(ethylene glycol)- block-poly( $\varepsilon$ -caprolactone)-block-poly(L-lysine) were also prepared.531
As a variation on tethering a platinum(IV) complex to a polymer backbone, the metal complex can itself act as a monomer for polymerization. In one instance, cis,cis,trans$\left[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{NH_{3}})_{2}\mathrm{Cl_{2}}(\bar{\mathrm{O}}_{2}\mathrm{CCH_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H})_{2}\right]$ was used as a monomer for condensation polymerization with ethylenediamine or piperazine.532 The corresponding condensation with diols proved unfruitful, but reaction of cis,cis,trans-[Pt$\mathrm{\hat{\l}N H_{3})_{2}C l_{2}(O H)_{2}]}$ with bifunctional anhydrides, such as cyclobutane tetraacetic anhydride, was able to give polyesters that could be further PEGylated to greatly enhance water solubility. The lack of blocky character in these polymers precluded their self-assembly into micellar nanoparticle structures. Another example of this strategy was the use of a cisplatin prodrug with pendent norbornenyl units at the axial positions that could serve to cross-link norbornene-terminated PEG chains, some of which were functionalized with either camptothecin or doxorubicin.533 The linkages to all three drug derivatives were chosen to enable differential release mechanisms of their payload: reduction, hydrolysis, or UV-irradiation.
An atypical method of platinum(IV) delivery using polymer nanoparticles is that in which the platinum complexes are not buried within the particle, but rather displayed on the surface in a manner somewhat analogous to that exhibited by nonpolymeric constructs such as SWCNTs and gold nanoparticles. Conjugation of cis,cis,trans- $\left[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{NH_{3}})_{2}\mathrm{Cl_{2}}(\mathrm{O\bar{H}})_{2}\right]$ to the succinate terminus of the amphiphilic molecule $\alpha$ -tocopheryl$\mathrm{PEG}_{1000}$ -succinate, which is conceptually identical to conj u g a t i o n o f c i s , c i s , t r a n s - $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{P t}\left(\dot{\mathbf{N}}\mathbf{H}_{3}\right)_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\left(\mathbf{OH}\right).$ - $(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CCH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H})$ ] to the hydrophilic portion of $\alpha$ - tocopheryl- $\mathrm{PEG}_{1000},$ produces a polymer that can self-assemble into a micelle that will present the platinum(IV) complex on its surface. Even though the platinum center is putatively attached to the nanostructure through a stable covalent linkage, an initial burst release occurred, followed by a longer period of more controlled release.534a In a subsequent development, this nanoparticle platform was stabilized by addition of $\alpha$ - tocopheryl- $\mathrm{PEG}_{1000}$ -succinylpoly(lactic acid), inclusion of which yielded surface-exposed carboxylic acids, to which the monoclonal antibody trastuzumab (Herceptin) was conjugated.534b This targeted nanoparticle, perhaps because of the difference in nanoparticle formation, showed a more controlled release profile than its predecessor.
## 8.8. Other Supramolecular Systems
The cisplatin-releasing platinum(IV) prodrug cis,cis,trans-[Pt$(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}\bar{\mathrm{Cl}}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})$ $\mathrm{\small{(O_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H)}}^{\prime}$ ] was conjugated to a phosphorylated oligopeptide that can act as a substrate for phosphatases.535 The molecule is designed to remain monomeric in circulation, but, upon entering tumor tissue where various phosphatases are overexpressed, enzymecatalyzed dephosphorylation will trigger self-assembly into supramolecular nanofibers. This self-assembly process was proposed to enhance retention of the prodrug at the site of disease. In vitro studies of the construct confirmed its ability to self-assemble into $\beta$ -sheet-like structures following action of alkaline phosphatase. The hydrogel formed by these nanofibers facilitated controlled release of active platinum(II) species upon incubation with chemical reductants. In vitro cytotoxicity studies confirmed that the compound can kill cultured murine and human cancer cells through induction of apoptosis. Mouse xenograft studies with subcutaneously grown murine breast cancers revealed enhanced tumor accumulation of the complex, comparable tumor growth inhibition, and lower systemic toxicity as compared to treatment with cisplatin.
Supramolecular cages with metal centers as vertices and rigid organic linkers as edges have long been investigated for their interesting chemical properties.536 These constructs are related to MOFs, but form discrete structures rather than extended networks. An octahedral hexanuclear cage was formed from six platinum(II) ethylenediamine units and four 2,4,6-tris(2- pyridyl)- $s$ -triazine molecules (Figure 8). The metal centers form the vertices of an octahedron, and the triazine units cover four faces of the polyhedron. The remaining four faces are open and provide access to the interior of the structure. The platinum(IV) prodrug cis,cis,trans- $\begin{array}{r}{[\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{OC}(\mathrm{O}).}\end{array}$ - $\mathrm{\bar{NHC}_{10}H_{15}})(\mathrm{O_{2}\bar{C}C H_{2}\bar{C}H_{2}C O_{2}H)]}$ (Figure 8) was designed to have one axial adamantyl unit and one trans succinate to act as a guest for the cage because four adamantyl groups can be loaded into the cage through the four open faces.537 The succinate ligands extend toward the solvent, enhancing water solubility, and at the same time offer a reactive handle for further functionalization; such functionalization was, however, not explored in this proof-of-principle study. The host−guest complex exhibited significantly improved intracellular accumulation and DNA platination as compared to the unencapsulated prodrug.
## 8.9. Proteins
In addition to the peptide targeting systems described in section 6, entire proteins have been used as drug delivery devices. The $\alpha$ -helical right-handed coiled coil (RHCC) is a 20- kDa portion of the tetrabrachion surface complex of Staphylothermus marinus.538 This extremophile colonizes exceedingly harsh environments, and the RHCC is correspondingly able to withstand extreme $\mathrm{\tt{pH}},$ , boiling temperatures, high pressures, and high salt concentrations.539 The hydrophobic pockets of this tetramer bind cisplatin and the resultant platinum-loaded self-assembled polypeptide structure was investigated as a drugdelivery vehicle.540 The protein-based construct did not elicit a significant immune response in mice, but the short half-life of the construct posed a significant challenge to further development. The authors subsequently investigated the ability of platinum(IV) species to be delivered by this tetramer.541 The chemical identity of the platinum species is not indicated in the paper; it is referred to as $\mathrm{^{\alpha}P t C l_{4}^{\mathrm{~\alpha\mathrm{~}},\mathrm{~}}}$ or “tetrachloro platinum $\mathrm{(IV)^{\dag}}$ from which we surmise that it is cis- $\mathrm{\big[Pt(NH_{3})_{2}C l_{4}\big]}$ , by extrapolation from their earlier cisplatin work. The construct is taken up by clathrin-mediated endocytosis and is effective in killing propagated cell lines and primary glioblastoma cells obtained from adult patients. Immunoblotting analyses indicated that apoptotic pathways were triggered by the construct. As compared to treatment with the unencapsulated platinum(IV) complex, intratumoral injection of the construct was better able to inhibit the growth of subcutaneous xenograft and intracerebral orthotopic tumors in mice.
Serum albumin is the most abundant protein in human blood plasma, and any intravenously administered drug will inevitably encounter it. In many cases, this interaction can sequester and deactivate the compound. In an effort to capitalize on the ability of human serum albumin (HSA) to act as a drug delivery vehicle, a platinum(IV) prodrug was designed to mimic the form of the fatty acids that this protein is known to bind. The complex cis,cis,trans- $\mathrm{\small{\left[{Pt(NH_{3})_{2}C l_{2}(O_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H)}}\right]_{2}}$ (OC$(\mathrm{O})\mathrm{\bar{N}H(C H_{2})_{15})C H_{3}]}$ interacts with HSA in a noncovalent, well-defined manner (Figure 9).542 A 1:1 complex of the platinum(IV) prodrug and HSA forms spontaneously on mixing and is sufficiently robust that it can be purified by fast protein liquid chromatography. Fluorescence quenching and modeling studies suggest that the platinum complex is buried beneath the surface of the protein and this encapsulation inhibits reduction by ascorbic acid. Significant enhancement in blood stability as compared to cisplatin or satraplatin was realized.
## 9. NANODELIVERY OF PLATINUM(II)
Many nanoconstructs designed to deliver platinum(II) complexes have also been described. These have recently been reviewed extensively by a number of different authors, $^{74,114,543-549}$ and we therefore do not provide a very detailed account of such constructs here. We would be remiss, however, if we did not include an overview of the significant clinical progress that has been made with certain macromolecular or self-assembled constructs that directly incorporate platinum(II) species.
